{"parentUuid":null,"isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"You are writing a research paper in LaTeX from completed experimental results. Produce a polished, compilable paper. Work in the repo /Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj.\n\n## Step 1 — READ these first (use the Read tool), in order:\n1. docs/dmitry/theory/writing_instructions.md — Neel Nanda's \"how to write ML papers\" advice. FOLLOW IT: a narrative of 1–3 concrete claims, rigorous evidence, motivate/contextualize, inform-not-persuade, no overclaiming, state limitations honestly, define terms.\n2. experiments/fra_win/RESULTS_SUMMARY.md — the compressed narrative + the 3 claims + the evidence. THIS IS YOUR SPINE.\n3. experiments/fra_win/THEORY.md — the converged mechanistic theory (cell decomposition, cell-vs-row/column expressivity argument, magnitude law, nonlinearity ledger, 5-clause win-checklist). Use this for the Theory/Method section.\n4. experiments/fra_win/CAMPAIGN_REPORT.md — all the experimental results, numbers, the win/loss taxonomy, the corrected metric, red-team verdicts (sprints 1 and 2). Pull exact numbers from here.\n5. experiments/fra_win/summary.md — the FIRST (induction) result + the sleeper bridge (this becomes an APPENDIX).\n6. docs/dmitry/theory/fra_writeup_tex/me/fra.tex — the EXISTING tex; COPY ITS STYLE (documentclass, geometry, fontenc, lmodern, microtype, amsmath/booktabs/hyperref-blue-links, the notation macros \\R \\E \\ip \\norm \\softmax \\dm \\dhead \\dsae \\OV \\QK etc., the theorem/proposition/definition/claim/remark environments, the Elhage-framework notation for attention). The new paper should look like it came from the same author.\n\n## Step 2 — WRITE the paper at docs/dmitry/theory/fra_paper_tex/fra_paper.tex\nReuse the preamble/macros from fra.tex (same packages, same notation conventions). Suggested structure (adapt as the narrative needs — narrative first, not a rigid template):\n- **Title**: something precise, e.g. \"When does Feature-Resolved Attention beat linear steering? A cell-level account and a mapped boundary.\" Author: Dmitry Manning-Coe. \\date{\\today}.\n- **Abstract**: the narrative + the 3 claims, concrete numbers (the 10^3–10^4× separability, the magnitude law, the boundary). One paragraph, tight.\n- **Introduction**: motivation (FRA is a beautiful microscope — is it ever the right *intervention* tool?); the 3 claims stated clearly; what's novel vs prior work; forward-reference the figures.\n- **Setup / FRA decomposition**: define the SAE basis, the exact cell decomposition S[q,k]=Σ_{μν} u^μ_q u^ν_k ω_{μν}, the cell as a multiplicative AND, the cell-level weight edit. Keep the Elhage notation from fra.tex. Include the nonlinearity ledger (RMSNorm scalar/exact, RoPE rotation/exact, Gemma soft-cap = the one true nonlinearity, GPT-2 LN-folding artifact) — as a Proposition or a compact subsection.\n- **Theory — why no linear steer can match it** (the core argument): score-space (a steer imitates a row/column of W_QK, never a cell, a fortiori never a cell-difference or family-union) + residual-broadcast (the write is read by every consumer). State the **magnitude law A ≈ reuse(marginal)/reuse(conjunction)** as a claim/proposition.\n- **The predictor (the win-checklist)**: CCF (content-conjunction fraction) + LBNR (load-bearing & non-redundant, causal head-finding) + the 5 clauses. Present as a clear checklist.\n- **Experiments**: (a) the CORRECTED fair metric (legit-content KL vs a content-gated projection-removal linear steer at matched on-target removal — explain WHY head-ablation/content-suppress are strawmen, what the sprint-1 red-team found); (b) the confirmed wins table with the legit-content KL numbers (retrieval 0.0016 vs 1.77 ~1100×; acronym ~0 vs 2.64 ~26,000×; copy-suppression natural A=516×; induction 15×; in-context backdoor 25×) — use booktabs; (c) the magnitude-law CURVE (Figure with A_generic flat 1.4→2.0 vs A_diff 6.3→13.4→23.8 over N=2..4); (d) the BOUNDARY MAP table (the failures: sleeper/greater-than/IOI/docstring/delimiter/jailbreak/PII-entity/class-union, each with the clause it isolates).\n- **Related work**: induction circuits (Olsson et al), copy-suppression (McDougall et al), retrieval heads (Wu et al), IOI (Wang et al), acronym circuit (Garcia-Carrasco et al), SAEs (Bricken/Templeton/Cunningham), activation/direction steering (ActAdd, refusal-is-a-direction Arditi et al, CAA), ACDC/attribution (Conmy et al). Be precise about what is and isn't novel.\n- **Limitations** (be honest, per the guidelines): the win-class is NARROW (most benchmark behaviors fall outside); gemma CCF sink-mask issue (CCF reliable on gpt2, behaviorally-certified via LBNR on gemma); SAE-explained-fraction/reach ceiling; small N on some external-validity; single-family of models (gpt2-small, gemma-2-2b); no ln1.hook_normalized SAE.\n- **Appendix A — The first FRA behavioral win (induction) and the sleeper bridge**: the ~15× induction-association result and the weight-baked-vs-in-context backdoor taxonomy from summary.md. Frame it as \"the existence proof this paper generalizes.\"\n- **Appendix B — methods/details**: the corrected-metric derivation, causal head-finding, differential cells, the magnitude-law experiment design.\n\n## Step 3 — FIGURES\nCopy the relevant figures into the paper folder and \\includegraphics them (use \\usepackage{graphicx}). Available in experiments/fra_win/figures/: fig_sprint2_summary.png (win/loss map + decisive metric — a key figure), fig_magnitude_law.png (the curve — key), fig6_retrieval.png (the separable×transferable 2x2), fig9_lbnr_tier2.png (LBNR gate + copy-suppression Tier-2), fig1_final.png (the induction win — for Appendix A). Copy them: `cp experiments/fra_win/figures/<f>.png docs/dmitry/theory/fra_paper_tex/`. Give each a clear, self-contained caption (captions are high-readership — explain the takeaway in the caption).\n\n## Step 4 — COMPILE\nCompile with `cd docs/dmitry/theory/fra_paper_tex && pdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode fra_paper.tex` (run twice for refs/toc). If pdflatex/latexmk is unavailable, leave the .tex clean and compilable and note it. Fix any LaTeX errors (undefined refs, missing packages — stick to common packages: geometry, amsmath, amssymb, amsthm, mathtools, booktabs, enumitem, xcolor, hyperref, graphicx, lmodern, microtype). Verify the PDF builds.\n\n## Quality bar\n- Narrative-driven, not a results dump. Lead with the claims. Quality over quantity of experiments.\n- Exact numbers from the source docs (don't invent). If unsure of a number, mark it or omit rather than fabricate.\n- Honest about the narrow win-class — that IS the central finding, present it as a strength (a precise boundary), not hidden.\n- Self-contained captions; define jargon; ~8–14 pages is fine.\n\nReturn: the path to the .tex, whether the PDF compiled, the section list, and any numbers you were unsure about or had to omit."},"uuid":"6ef8321c-6898-41c5-a6e2-645988f186c5","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:24.467Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
