{"parentUuid":"4f1a1a7c-bcfc-4e98-8301-bd9eaad26d67","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ada392f8670716699","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01XrbeDhVEJ744JJxhsVrqDo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PB8yZWQhPhZtJD4vqCwZAf","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/RESULTS_SUMMARY.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6965,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12183,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":228,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12183},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6965,"output_tokens":228,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12183,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":12183,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cbx7GMGDkHkWwsLPF2zyU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"be11eba4-ac20-42d9-b37b-ee4fc1a9aeda","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:28.652Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"4f1a1a7c-bcfc-4e98-8301-bd9eaad26d67","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XPbQBfc69GX1ruJx8sAp6D","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\n2\tHighly Opinionated Advice on How to Write ML Papers\n3\tby Neel Nanda\n4\t12th May 2025\n5\tTL;DR\n6\tThe essence of an ideal paper is the narrative: a short, rigorous and evidence-based technical story you tell, with a takeaway the readers care about\n7\tWhat? A narrative is fundamentally about a contribution to our body of knowledge: one to three specific novel claims that fit within a cohesive theme\n8\tWhy? You need rigorous empirical evidence that convincingly supports your claims\n9\tSo what? Why should the reader care?\n10\tWhat is the motivation, the problem you’re trying to solve, the way it all fits in the bigger picture?\n11\tWhat is the impact? Why does your takeaway matter? The north star of a paper is ensuring the reader understands and remembers the narrative, and believes that the paper’s evidence supports it\n12\tThe first step is to compress your research into these claims.\n13\tThe paper must clearly motivate these claims, explain them on an intuitive and technical level, and contextualise what’s novel in terms of the prior literature\n14\tThis is the role of the abstract & introduction\n15\tExperimental Evidence: This is absolutely crucial to get right and aggressively red-team, it’s how you resist the temptation of elegant but false narratives.\n16\tQuality > Quantity: find compelling experiments, not a ton of vaguely relevant ones.\n17\tThe experiments and results must be explained in full technical detail - start high-level in the intro/abstract, show results in figures, and get increasingly detailed in the main body and appendix.\n18\tEnsure researchers can check your work - provide sufficient detail to be replicated\n19\tDefine key terms and techniques - readers have less context than you think.\n20\tWrite iteratively: Write abstract -> bullet point outline -> introduction -> first full draft -> repeat\n21\tGet feedback and reflect after each stage\n22\tSpend comparable amounts of time on each of: the abstract, the intro, the figures, and everything else - they have about the same number_of_readers * time_to_read\n23\tInform, not persuade: Avoid the trap of overclaiming or ignoring limitations. Scientific integrity may get you less hype, but gains respect from the researchers who matter.\n24\tPrecision, not obfuscation: Use jargon where needed to precisely state your point, but not for the sake of sounding smart. Use simple language wherever possible.\n25\t\n26\t\n27\tCase study: The abstract of refusal is mediated by a single direction, broken down into the purpose of each sentence\n28\t\n29\tIntroduction\n30\tYour research only matters if people read, understand, and build upon it. This means that writing a good paper is a critical part of the research process. Further, the process of writing forces you to clarify your own thinking in ways that often reveal gaps or new insights - I’ve often only properly understood an idea after writing it up. Yet, to many, writing feels less fun than research and is treated as an after thought - a common but critical mistake.\n31\t\n32\tIn my experience supervising 20+ papers and reading/appreciating/being annoyed by a bunch more, I've developed my own opinionated framework for what I think makes a paper good and how to approach the writing process. I try to lay this out in this post, along with a bunch of concrete advice.[1] This post assumes you’ve already done a bunch of technical research, and focuses on how to effectively share it with the world, see my other posts for advice on the research part.\n33\t\n34\tCaveat: I mostly have experience with writing mechanistic interpretability papers and this advice is written with that flavour. I expect much of it to generalise to the rest of ML and some to generalise to other fields but it's hard for me to say. Further, this is very much my personal opinionated, and optimised more for truth-seeking than getting into conferences[2]. See other great advice here and here.\n35\t\n36\tCaveat 2: There are many reasonable objections to the academic paper as the format for communicating research. Alas, engaging with those is outside the scope of this post, it’s long enough as it is.\n37\t\n38\tThe Essence of a Paper\n39\tAt its core, a paper should present a narrative of one to three specific concrete claims that you believe to be true, that build to some useful takeaway(s). Everything else in the paper exists to support this narrative. The second pillar of the paper is rigorous evidence for why they are true - obviously there will always be some chance you’re wrong, but they should be compelling and believable, without obvious glaring flaws.\n40\t\n41\tCommunicate the key idea\n42\tMotivate why someone should care about them\n43\tContextualize them in existing literature\n44\tCommunicate them precisely, with all relevant technical detail, terminology and background context\n45\tProvide sufficient evidence to support them\n46\tCrafting a Narrative\n47\tOne of the critical steps that can make or break a paper is crafting a narrative. What does this actually mean? And how can you do it?\n48\t\n49\tResearch is about discovering new things and pushing forward our frontier of existing knowledge. I view a paper as something that finds insight and provides compelling evidence behind it. The way to tell when you could start writing a paper is when you have learned something insightful, in a way that could be made legible to someone else.\n50\t\n51\tThis is far easier said than done. A research project is often a mess of fun results, confusions, insights, and remaining mysteries. Even when you’ve made enough progress to write it up, you will likely have a great deal of tacit knowledge, interesting rabbit holes, dangling threads, etc - projects rarely feel done.\n52\t\n53\tI find that converting a project into a great narrative is a subtle and difficult skill, one of the many facets of research taste. It’s something I’ve gotten much, much better at over time, and it’s hard to say what the best way to get better at it is, beyond experience. One exercise I’d recommend is taking papers you know, and trying to write down what their narrative is, and ask yourself what its strengths and weaknesses are. If at all possible, consult mentors/more experienced researchers for advice. But if you need to come up with one yourself, the right questions to ask look like:\n54\t\n55\tWhich of these results would be most exciting to show someone?\n56\tActually show someone your findings and ask what they're most interested in\n57\tWhat seems particularly important?\n58\tWhy should anyone care about this work?\n59\tWhat was hard about what you did, that perhaps no one else has done?\n60\tA good, compelling narrative comes with motivation and impact. The key points to be sure to cover:\n61\t\n62\tThe context of your insight\n63\tThe problem you're trying to solve\n64\tWhy this matters\n65\tWhat you have shown\n66\tWhy the reader should believe it\n67\tWhat the insight is\n68\tWhy do you need this kind of compressed narrative? Often there’s far more insight in a research project than can be contained in this structure. But it is impossible to convey this level of nuance in a paper. Readers will rarely take away more than a few sentences of content. Choose those sentences carefully. These are the insights shared by your paper, your contribution to the literature. These are the specific, concrete claims that you want to communicate - you cannot reliably communicate much more. You will need to compress your research findings down into a handful of claims, prioritise those, and accept that you may need to drop a bunch of other detail, or move it to appendices. If you don’t deliberately de-prioritise some details, then something else will get dropped, which may have been far more important.\n69\t\n70\tWhat do I mean by claims? For example:\n71\t\n72\t\"Method X is the best approach on task Y (according to metric Z)\"\n73\t\"A substantial part of the model's behavior in scenario A is explained by simple explanation B\"\n74\t\"Technique C can fail in scenario D if conditions E and F hold\"\n75\tOne important claim, with sufficiently strong evidence, can be enough for a great paper! If you want multiple claims, I strongly recommend choosing claims that fit together in a cohesive theme - papers are far easier to understand, praise, share, etc if there is a coherent narrative, not just a grab-bag of unconnected ideas.\n76\t\n77\tDepending on the strength of the evidence, you can adjust the confidence of a claim:\n78\t\n79\tExistence-proof claims: \"We found at least one example where X happens\" (like the indirect object identification paper providing an existence-proof for self-repair)\n80\tSystematic claims: \"X generally happens across a wide range of contexts\" or \"X is common\"\n81\tHedged claims: “There is compelling/suggestive/tentative evidence that X is true”\n82\tNarrow claims: “X is the best method for specific situations V & W, if your goal is objective Y”\n83\tGuarantees: “X is always true”[3]\n84\t\n85\tGenerally, stronger statements make for more interesting papers, but require higher standards of evidence - resist the temptation to overclaim for clicks!\n86\t\n87\tWhen to Start?\n88\tAnother thorny question is: When should you stop doing research and start writing up your research? This is a hard and subtle question that is, in many ways, a matter of research taste, but here is my general guide:\n89\t\n90\tWrite down a list of things you've learned\n91\tReview that list carefully, and ideally show it to someone else\n92\tAsk yourself how comfortable you would be defending the claim that you have provided meaningful, positive evidence for these results\n93\tThink about reasons why others might care about this\n94\tFocus on things you've done that have been hard or non-trivial and look for exciting elements\n95\tBut generally, this is unfortunately just a hard thing to tell when starting out, and gets far easier with time and experience. If you can consult a more experienced researcher, definitely do.\n96\t\n97\tA more meta piece of advice when starting out is to try to choose projects where the narrative will be pretty obvious, e.g. method X beats SOTA method Y in domain Z on metric W\n98\t\n99\tWarning: Before moving into paper-writing mode, it's crucial to verify that your evidence is actually correct. An unfortunate fact is that many published papers are basically false or wildly misleading. Don't let this happen to you! Carefully check your critical experiments and, if possible, re-implement them through alternate pathways. Ideally, verify all experiments worth mentioning in the paper, or at least 75% of them.\n100\t\n101\tNovelty\n102\tA common and confusing requirement for papers is that the results be novel, something that is not covered before. What exactly does this mean? Science is fundamentally about building a large body of knowledge. This means that your work exists in the context of what has come before. Novelty means it expands our knowledge.\n103\t\n104\tThe conventional definition of novelty can be annoying and, in my opinion, focuses too much on shininess and doesn't capture the more important aspect of whether our knowledge has expanded. Another way to put this is: Should I assign different probabilities to propositions I care about after observing the results of this paper?\n105\t\n106\tRigorous, at-scale replications of shaky results, negative results of seemingly promising hypotheses, and high-quality failed replications of popular papers are all very valuable contributions. I would personally consider these novel because they expand our knowledge. However, the revealed preferences of many reviewers and researchers suggest they do not feel the same way. Such is life.\n107\t\n108\tI don’t want to go too far re criticising novelty: there are many cases where I am uninterested in a paper due to lack of novelty. This primarily occurs with methods that I expect to work when applied in standard settings, and I assign a high probability of success, so the project provides few bits of information. While knowing that such a method failed could be interesting, projects can also fail due to researcher incompetence or bad luck. Therefore, it is difficult to draw meaningful conclusions without evidence of researcher competence.\n109\t\n110\tLeaving that aside, novelty can be hard to communicate. Given a paper on its own, it's difficult to tell what is and is not supposed to be novel:\n111\t\n112\tAre the techniques used innovative or just standard techniques?\n113\tDoes the claim represent a deep conceptual breakthrough?\n114\tIs it a very simple extension of standard ideas?\n115\tIs it a natural consequence of a more ambitious claim put forward in a different piece of work?\n116\tThe main way to address this is to be extremely clear about what is and is not novel, especially in the introduction and related work, and to liberally cite the most relevant papers and explain why your work is and is not different.\n117\t\n118\tHow to find out what came before? Use a large language model. If you’re not already familiar with a relevant literature, LLMs are pretty great at doing quick literature reviews, e.g. Gemini Deep Research[4]. Reading the literature yourself is much better of course, but takes way, way longer and should have been done at the start project.\n119\t\n120\tOne reason this is very important is that, depending on what’s claimed as novel, the same paper could be perceived as either inappropriately arrogant or making a modest incremental contribution, depending on how the claims are presented.\n121\t\n122\tContextualizing your work within existing literature is particularly crucial for experienced researchers who are familiar with the field. Clear explanation in the introduction helps them quickly engage with your work and see what’s interesting, else it blurs into all other superficially similar papers they’ve read and doesn’t seem worth the effort.\n123\t\n124\tThere are a few problems with novelty as it is traditionally thought of\n125\t\n126\tNovelty is often overemphasized - it incentivises going for ambitious but shaky claims over simple and rigorous insights.\n127\tThis can mean that if there's an existing paper that provides a preliminary but shaky case for a claim, going and doing it properly can seem less exciting, even though this is in some ways a more useful scientific contribution, as it establishes a confident foundation for others to build upon.\n128\tAnother complex question arises when you have legitimate complaints about prior work, and your work superficially looks derivative, but this is because you identified a significant methodological flaw or bug.\n129\tI recommend being clear that you have criticism, but it's important to remain professional while explaining what was flawed and why this matters, and how your work resolves it, without critiquing the authors or their motivations.\n130\tThere are various social norms that are kind of annoying, such as citing being obliged to cite the first instance of a concept (even if later iterations are much clearer) and referencing a ton of vaguely relevant work even if it adds nothing to the paper - people can get offended if not cited. But this doesn’t detract from all the ways that citations genuinely strengthen a paper\n131\tI personally prefer to just do work that is optimised for scientific value, and shoe-horn it into a peer review friendly lens at the end, if applicable. But I’m in a fortunate position here, and there are real career incentives around getting published.\n132\t\n133\tTwo example papers of mine where being clear about novelty was tricky:\n134\t\n135\tIn my Othello work, I built directly on Kenneth Lee's paper that showed an Othello plane model had a world model found with non-linear probes. My contribution was demonstrating that it could be found with linear probes, which was interesting for a bunch of reasons to do with the linear representation hypothesis, but I needed to be careful to not claim credit for anything Kenneth did\n136\tIn my refusal paper, our key result was that refusal is mediated by a single direction. But it was not novel to find that a concept was linearly represented, the significant part was doing it for refusal: a particularly interesting concept.\n137\tOther work had loosely tried to do this for refusal, but had less compelling results, so we had to explain why our’s was better (much larger effect sizes, more models, downstream tasks, etc)\n138\tWe also showed that we could now jailbreak the model by removing this direction from the weights - the novelty was less that we could jailbreak models (that's already known to be easy with finetuning), but that we could do it with interpretability tools, and so cheaply, one of the first practical applications of interpretability (even if, you know, not quite for safety...)\n139\tRigorous Supporting Evidence\n140\tA paper is worth little unless it can convince[5] the reader of its key claims. To do this, you need evidence. In machine learning, this typically means experiments. Below, I discuss how I think about what good experimental evidence looks like - see my research process sequence for more advice.\n141\t\n142\tWith claims the priority is being able to communicate the intuitions to everyone, but with experiments the priority is being able to justify it in full technical detail to an engaged, skeptical reader. You also want to explain what’s going on intuitively, to support your claims, but this is less key than actually having good, legitimate evidence.\n143\t\n144\tA particularly important thing to get right is extensive red-teaming: you should spend a good amount of your time, both during the original research and now, red teaming your narrative. One of the main traps introduced by the framing of “find a great narrative” is the temptation to ignore inconvenient contradictory results - don’t let this happen to you. Tips:\n145\t\n146\tAssume you've made a mistake - what is that mistake? Assume there's a hole in your case that your evidence supports your grand narrative - where is that hole? Try to break it apart.\n147\tTry to get other researchers, especially more experienced ones, to weigh in.\n148\tMake sure to extensively discuss limitations. If you notice issues, design and perform new experiments to test for them. This is all the more important the more ambitious or surprising your claims are.\n149\tWhen I read a paper with a bold claim, I have a strong prior that it is false, and I am constantly looking for holes. If I identify one, and the authors have not checked whether that is a real flaw, I will generally move on.\n150\tHowever, if they have preempted me and provide sufficient evidence that I can be confident it's not a flaw, then those papers can be incredibly exciting and insightful.\n151\tWhat does good evidence look like?\n152\t\n153\tGood experiments distinguish between hypotheses: Often, you will have several plausible hypotheses for some phenomena. The point of an experiment is to have results that vary significantly depending on which is true (i.e. that provide Bayesian evidence) - if the results vary enough, and the experiment is sufficiently reliable, then one good experiment can falsify many hypotheses\n154\tCan you trust your results?:\n155\tHow reliable is my experiment? Ask yourself: \"How surprised would I be if it turned out to be complete bullshit due to a bug, error, noise, misunderstanding, etc.?\" Investigate the most uncertain bits\n156\tHow noisy is my experiment? If you ran similar experiments several times, how confident would you be that the results would be consistent? What is your sample size? What is your standard deviation? Are your results clearly distinguishable from noise? (There's a whole host of statistical theory here; your favourite LLM can probably effectively teach you about the basics.)\n157\tStatistical rigour: If you're doing some type of frequentist test[6], you probably shouldn't use p < .05 as a threshold. In stats heavy fields, like the social sciences, papers that report their central finding at .01 < p < .05, usually fail to replicate. If you're doing an exploratory approach, you should be skeptical of any result that isn't p < .001, as the number of possible hypotheses is vast.\n158\t\n159\tPrior work discussing replicability is very strict on this point: \"One prior study of 103 replication attempts [in psychology] indeed found a 74% replication rate for findings reported at p ≤ .005 and a 28% replication rate for findings at .005 < p < .05 (Gordon et al., 2021)\". There are also various statistical reasons why true findings usually won't produce .01 < p < .05.[7]\n160\t\n161\tThis skepticism and sanity checking is especially key for particularly surprising or novel bits of evidence. Wherever possible, I will try to re-implement a key experiment from scratch or try to get at the same evidence via a somewhat different route, just to make sure that I'm not missing something crucial.\n162\tAblation studies: When a paper introduces a complex new method, there are often several moving parts. For example, they may make changes A, B, and C to standard practice. If they then only evaluate the standard method or the method with all three changes, it's impossible to tell which changes are actually effective and necessary. It's good practice to remove one change at a time, observe its effect, and then repeat this process for each change.\n163\tUnknown Unknowns: How confident are you that there isn't some alternative explanation for your results that you're missing?\n164\tThis is a gnarly one. You'll want to think hard about it, ideally ask other people for feedback and get their perspectives. However, ultimately, you may sometimes just need to move on after a reasonable effort and accept that you may have missed something.\n165\tAvoiding Misleading Evidence (Cherry-Picking and Post-Hoc Analysis):\n166\tWas this cherry-picked? Researchers can, accidentally or purposefully, produce evidence that looks more compelling than it actually is. One classic way is cherry-picking: presenting only the examples that look most compelling. This is particularly dangerous with qualitative evidence, like case studies.\n167\tWhile qualitative evidence can be extremely valuable, it’s important to note how cherry-picked it was. Ideally, provide randomly selected examples for context to give a fairer picture.\n168\tThe main exception is if your claim is an existence proof. In this case, one example suffices, if it’s a trustworthy result.\n169\tTrack pre/post-hoc analysis. It's important to clearly track which experimental results were obtained before versus after you formulated your claim. Post-hoc analysis (interpreting results after they're seen) is inherently less impressive than predictions confirmed by pre-specified experiments.\n170\tBe aware that even complex predictions suggested by a hypothesis can turn out to be correct for the wrong reasons\n171\tFor example, in a toy model of universality, I came up with the key representation theory-based algorithm I thought the network would follow before we got our key pieces of empirical evidence. I felt very confident. However, follow-up work found that a different explanation, which also involved representation theory, was what was actually occurring.\n172\tQuality Over Quantity: Try to prioritise having at least one really compelling and hard to deny experiment, over a bunch of mediocre ones.\n173\tIf you do have many experiments, often some are more compelling than others. Highlight the ones that most strongly support your claims in the main text and consider moving others to an appendix or referencing them more briefly.\n174\tDiverse Lines of Evidence Are Robust: On the flip side, it can be far better to have several qualitatively different lines of evidence all pointing to the same conclusion, rather than many very similar experiments that all use similar methodologies and standards of proof.\n175\tQualitatively different basically means “given the result of experiment 1, how well can I predict the result of experiment 2?”\n176\tThis can justify putting effort into weak lines of evidence; for example, qualitative analysis of some data points can be useful supporting evidence of a quantitative study, even if insufficient to carry a paper independently, as they make it less likely that the summary statistics hid a subtle flaw.\n177\tDistilling Experiments:\n178\tOften, at the end of a project, you'll have run many experiments, some of which felt around the edges of your core claims. But by this stage, you likely have a much clearer idea of what the most promising kinds of evidence are. If practical (considering time and resources), consider going back to run a more conclusive, decisive experiment using what you now know.\n179\tThis could also involve scaling up: using more models, larger sample sizes, sweeping hyperparameters more thoroughly, running on more diverse datasets, etc.\n180\tBaselines are Crucial:\n181\tA common mistake is for people to try to show a technique works by demonstrating it gets \"decent\" results, rather than showing it achieves better results than plausible alternatives that people might have used or are standard in the field.\n182\tImplicitly you’re supporting the weak claim “method X works at all” not “method X is actually worth using in practice”\n183\tSadly this is especially prevalent in fields like mechanistic interpretability, where the comparative need for qualitative evidence can lead to neglecting more rigorous and systematic quantitative comparisons against strong baselines - the best papers have both qualitative and quantitative evidence.\n184\tThe subtlety of baselines: It's not enough to just have them; you must strive to have the strongest possible baselines. Put meaningful effort into making them good. Often, a \"competitor\" method can seem weak but can significantly improve with proper hyperparameter tuning, prompt engineering, or appropriate scaffolding.\n185\tThere's a natural bias to invest more effort in making one's \"cool, shiny\" new technique look good than in optimizing \"boring\" baselines. Resist this. Rigorous comparison to strong baselines is critical for good science and for genuinely persuading informed readers.\n186\tThe Guiding Question for Evidence:\n187\tUltimately, the question to ask about your evidence is: \"Should this update a reader's beliefs about my claims?\" not \"Does this fit the stereotypical picture of a rigorous academic paper?\" While the latter often correlates with the former, your primary goal is genuine persuasion through sound evidence.\n188\tEg if reading several dataset examples by hand is genuinely strong evidence of your claim, just report that and justify why it’s great evidence!\n189\tReproducibility & Publishing code: Rigour can be in the eye of the beholder: if readers cannot understand or verify it for themselves, it’s far harder to consider it rigorous. So your paper will be made substantially more useful by providing more detail about your exact methods.\n190\tA particularly useful approach is sharing your code. This enables others to build on your work and clarifies any ambiguities left in the paper (there will always be some). More broadly, it provides transparency into your exact process.\n191\tIf you have time, you should:\n192\tEnsure the codebase runs on a fresh machine\n193\tWrite a helpful README that includes links to key resources like model weights or datasets (which can be easily hosted on Hugging Face)\n194\tCreate a Python notebook demonstrating how to run the key components\n195\tTragically, the world is complicated, and there is often no single clear recipe to deal with all edge cases in research. These considerations are guidelines to help navigate that complexity\n196\t\n197\tPaper Structure Summary\n198\tHow are these claims and experiments translated into a paper?\n199\t\n200\tAbstract: Motivate the paper, present your narrative and the impact: explain your key claims and why you believe they are true - be as concise and high-level as possible, while still getting the point across. Give enough information for a reader to understand the key takeaways of your paper and whether to read further or not - they often won’t read further, so it’s key that they still get the gist!\n201\tThe reader is coming in from a cold-start, and may have no idea what your paper is about - you need to help them orient fast, and indicate what “genre” your paper fits into\n202\tThe rest of your paper exists to support the abstract\n203\tIntroduction: Basically an extended abstract that fleshes out your narrative - explain your key claims, motivate them, contextualise them in the key parts of the existing literature. Explain your key experiments and the results and why this supports your claims. Ensure the reader leaves understanding the narrative of the paper, and whether to read further or not - they often won’t. This is basically a self-contained paper summary, don’t worry about “spoiling” the paper or repetition - with a complex idea, you want to repeat it in varied ways so that it sticks.\n204\tThe introduction sets the structure of the rest of the paper\n205\tMain content: This is where the real technical detail lives. Clearly and precisely explain background concepts and results, your precise claims, what exactly you did for your experiments (in full detail, using appendices if need be, relevant baselines, etc), the results, what they mean and their implications, etc.\n206\tThis should be tightly planned to support the key claims, not sprawling and comprehensive. For section and subsection you should have a clear answer for how it contributes to the narrative, and would be damaging to remove.\n207\tI recommend first planning out clear opening and closing sentences of each paragraph: what does the paragraph show and how does it fit into the paper?\n208\tFigures: Figures and tables are a key medium for communicating experimental results. Diagrams are great for communicating key ideas and claims. Put a lot of effort into your figures.\n209\tGood captions are also crucial - you need to given context on what the figure shows, the nuance and intended interpretation, and key technical detail. Ideally the reader will understand everything from just the figure and just the caption, though this is ambitious\n210\tRelated work: This is a mini literature review - I generally put this after the main content and don’t think it’s super important. Giving context on a few key similar papers and how your work differs is crucial, but typically done in the intro.\n211\tDiscussion (/limitations/conclusion/etc): A place to put all the high-level reflections - limitations of your work, future work, key implications, etc. This is not essential, but is nice if you have something worthwhile to say. Acknowledging key limitations is very important, and papers that don’t do this are substantially weaker and less useful (in my opinion).\n212\tAppendices: Everything else - in the main paper you need to care about being concise, but here you can do whatever you want. Often you want to briefly discuss something in the main paper and move all technical detail to the appendix. Appendices are pretty low stakes and rarely read except by superfans, so don’t stress them too much.\n213\tAnalysing My Grokking Work\n214\tThis is all pretty abstract. To concretise this, let’s look at my grokking paper through this lens. I’ve broken it down into claims, evidence, context and motivation, with commentary thrown in. This is somewhat stylised for pedagogical reasons, but hopefully useful!\n215\t\n216\tMeta: This was a challenging paper to write!\n217\tThere was significant technical detail to our claims and evidence, largely unfamiliar to readers - mech interp was very new, and we did something weird and novel. We needed to communicate our claims (the algorithm) and our experimental evidence, and justify why the evidence was believable, since there were no standard methods to follow\n218\tA good diagram was critical to explaining the algorithm:\n219\t\n220\t\n221\tFigure 1 from the paper, and lead image in the tweet thread\n222\t\n223\tThis was more like two papers - the reverse engineering, and the study of circuit formation, and we needed to compress both into the same page limit. Fortunately, they did fit a cohesive theme\n224\tStructure:\n225\t\n226\tClaim: We fully reverse engineered a tiny transformer trained on modular addition\n227\tMeta: This is a general claim, but about a specific model\n228\tContext: We needed to explain the entire notion of reverse-engineering a model from its weights, as readers may not have been familiar. The motivation for why this is interesting is pretty obvious, but the goal is easy to misunderstand\n229\tEvidence: We show this with several lines of evidence: activation and weight analysis, and causal interventions\n230\tClaim: This circuit forms gradually, well before the point of sudden grokking -> grokking is a gradual from memorisation followed by removing memorisation\n231\tContext: The entire notion of grokking as covered in prior work\n232\tMeta: This is critical context to understand my paper, but is also prior work - I need to explain enough detail for unfamiliar readers to follow, but without excessive repetition, and cite it for them to see further details.\n233\tMotivation: Grokking is a big deal and surprised many people. We show it’s fairly different from what people think.\n234\tEvidence: We show this by designing convincing progress measures to track the circuit, and show that they shift well before grokking\n235\tThe Writing Process: Compress then Iteratively Expand\n236\tNote: Check out my paper writing checklist for a concrete to-do list of what I recommend doing here\n237\t\n238\tSo, you have a list of claims and key experiments. Now, all you need to do is write the paper! I recommend an iterative process - start with a bullet point narrative, then a full bullet point outline, then flesh it out into prose, taking time to reflect at each stage. See above or the next section for more details on the actual structure of a paper, here I just try to convey the high-level strategy\n239\t\n240\tA key challenge in paper writing is the illusion of transparency - you have spent months steeped in the context of this research project. You have tons of context, your reader does not. You’ll need to help them understand exactly what you are doing, in the large space of all possible ML papers, and address all the misconceptions and possible misunderstandings, even though to you it all feels obvious. This is a difficult skill - wherever possible, get extensive feedback from others to address\n241\t\n242\tSpend far more time on early sections: Realistically, tons of people read the title of your paper, many read the abstract, some read the introduction/skim the figures, and occasionally they read the whole thing. This means you should spend about the same amount of time on each of: the abstract, the intro, the figures, and everything else[8]. (I’m only half joking)\n243\t\n244\tCompress\n245\tYou should start by compressing your work as much as possible. Some tips:\n246\t\n247\tVerbally describe it to someone.\n248\tBonus: Ask them what was most interesting, or to repeat it back to you\n249\tPlan out a talk.\n250\tGive your research notes to an LLM and ask it to summarize the key points.\n251\tAfter each, think about what's missing, what's extraneous, what’s inaccurate or misleading, and iterate.\n252\tThis compression step is crucial because it forces you to identify:\n253\t\n254\tThe 1-3 concrete claims you believe to be true\n255\tWhy these claims matter (brief motivation)\n256\tThe crucial experimental evidence for each claim (ideally 1-3 key experiments per claim)\n257\tNext, critically evaluate this compressed version:\n258\t\n259\tDo your experiments genuinely support your claims?\n260\tAre there ways your experimental evidence could be flawed or misinterpreted?\n261\tCould the evidence be true but the claim false? How?\n262\tAs part of this process, write down common misconceptions, limitations, or ways someone might over-update on your work.\n263\t\n264\tIteratively Expand\n265\tOnce you have a compressed list of bullet points that you are satisfied with, you should start iteratively expanding and developing them. After each step, stop, reflect, read through, and edit - rushing a step can lead to a lot of wasted time at the next step.\n266\t\n267\tIf you have a research supervisor/mentor, it is very valuable to get feedback at each stage - I find it way faster and easier to give feedback on a narrative or bullet point outline than being sent 8 pages of dense prose! Even if you don’t have a mentor, try to get feedback from someone[9].\n268\t\n269\tStart with the compressed bullet point narrative - make sure you’re happy that this captures the narrative you want!\n270\tWrite a bullet point outline of the introduction - the north star here is to communicate what your claims are, exactly, (including which parts are novel vs building on prior work), why they matter, and a high-level idea of why they are true\n271\tThis involves more detail, key citations to the literature, more detailed motivations, etc. Generally it won’t get too technical, but can involve explaining a few crucial concepts.\n272\tFlow matters a lot here! Try to get feedback on how it feels to an unfamiliar reader, and how cohesive it feels\n273\tWrite a bullet point outline of the full paper - covering the key experiments, results, methodology, background, limitations, etc\n274\tThe north star is to convince a skeptical, engaged reader that your claims are true - give them enough information to understand your experiments and the results\n275\tA good outline is tight and minimal - every part of it should have a clear role in the overall narrative. If you don’t have a good answer to “what goes wrong if I cut this”, you should cut it.\n276\tGood figures are crucial to communicate results - plan these out, but leave making them to step 4\n277\tThis can include writing the related work, or you can leave that to the end.\n278\tResults: Collect key experimental results and make first draft figures to show. Does this convincingly support your narrative? What’s missing? Which parts and complex and need more exposition, vs standard/unsurprising and can be sped through?\n279\tYou can’t always get this done in advance, but it’s much better if you do - more time to refine, iterate, etc.\n280\tFirst draft: Flesh this out into prose and full technical detail\n281\tIf you have writer’s block, try giving an LLM your outline, some relevant papers, and asking for a first draft. Do not just copy this into your paper, but I find that sometimes LLMs have good ideas, and that frustration with poor quality LLM write-ups can be a great way to break through writer’s block.\n282\tEdit it: Repeatedly pass over your first draft (and get feedback), clean it up, polish it, make the narrative as tight and clear as possible, cut out extraneous fluff, make the figures glorious, etc\n283\tThis is worth spending a lot of time on, it can make a big difference!\n284\tThe Anatomy of a Paper\n285\tOK, so what actually goes into a paper? What are the key components you’ll need to write, and what is the point of each?\n286\t\n287\tAbstract\n288\tCheck out the annotated abstract earlier for a concrete breakdown\n289\t\n290\tAn abstract should give a cold-start reader a sense of what the paper is about - what sub-field, what type of paper, what key motivating questions, etc. This is a key manifestation of the illusion of transparency:  you know exactly what your project is about but to your reader there is a large space of possibilities, and without any context may have completely incorrect priors and wildly misinterpret.\n291\t\n292\tPeople will often leave your abstract then move on, unless strongly compelled - it’s a big deal to get right, and deserves high polish\n293\t\n294\tA common approach is:\n295\t\n296\tFirst sentence: Something uncontroversially true that clearly states which part of ML you're focused on (e.g., \"Thinking models have recently become state-of-the-art across many reasoning tasks.\")\n297\tSecond sentence: Something that makes clear there's a need, something unknown, or a problem for your paper to solve (e.g., \"The transition to reasoning models raises novel challenges for interpretability.\") - this should convey (some of) the motivation\n298\tNow the reader is situated, you need to concisely communicate your claims. Again, illusion of transparency - they often won’t know your techniques, the work you’re building on, key ideas, etc. Abstracts should be as accessible as possible - use simple language as much as you can\n299\t\n300\tSentence 3: State the crucial contribution of this paper and why it is exciting - you’ll need to lose nuance, this is OK.\n301\tOptional: Sentence 4 should provide clarifying details on that claim, such as its meaning and how evidence could be provided if not obvious.\n302\tInclude key definitions for any necessary jargon, though jargon should be avoided if possible, unless it’s standard in the field and useful to contextualise the paper within the field.\n303\tEach of the next few sentences should focus on either key experimental evidence or additional important claims. These can sometimes overlap, where a specific claim being true also supports the main claim.\n304\tTry to have 1 sentence per idea - this forces you to be concise, without getting overwhelming.\n305\tIf possible, include a concrete metric or result in any of the above that gives readers a sense that your results are real and substantial.\n306\tThis can look like folding in key evidence of a claim into the sentence introducing the claim.\n307\tFinally, close with motivation:\n308\t\n309\tFinal 1-2 sentences: Wrap up by reminding readers why the paper matters/is a big deal, its implications, and how it fits into the broader context.\n310\tThis is also a good place to clearly state your standard of evidence, whether your work is:\n311\tA preliminary step towards…\n312\tShows that method X should be used in practice\n313\tShows that practitioners should take care when using method Y\n314\tEstablishes best practices for Z\n315\tProvides compelling evidence that…\n316\tIntroduction\n317\tThe introduction is broadly similar to the abstract but more extended and in-depth. I proceed in roughly this order:\n318\t\n319\tParagraph 1: Context - What topic are we studying, what is the key motivating question, and why does it matter?\n320\tOptionally: 1 sentence on how our contribution answers it\n321\tIt’s good to liberally cite papers here to establish things like ‘this is a real field’, ‘this problem matters’, ‘people are interested in it and have tried (and failed) to solve it/have solved variants’\n322\tParagraph 2: Technical background - what do we know about this problem? What are the established techniques our paper rests on? Etc\n323\tIt’s good to cite liberally here to establish that what you’re using are standard methods and concepts, and to give the reader more context.\n324\tHere and in paragraph 1 you want to better situate your problem in the broader strategic picture of the field. Why does this matter? What other work has been done here, and why is it inadequate?\n325\tParagraph 3: Key contribution - What exactly is our main claim? Add key nuance, detail, context, etc.\n326\tParagraph 3.5[10]: Our case - summarise the most critical evidence we provide that our main claim is true\n327\t\n328\t[Optional]: More paragraphs for a second or third claim and the key case\n329\tParagraph 4: Impact - What should you take away from this paper? What are the implications, why is it a big deal, who should take different actions as a result of the results, etc. This may be emphasising practical utility, pushing forwards basic science, correcting common misconceptions, etc.\n330\tContributions: End with a bullet point list of concise descriptions of your key claims, ideally with concise descriptions of key evidence\n331\tYou want something a reader can look at and decide if they’re impressed/interested\n332\tNote: Citing here isn't about performatively covering all the relevant papers[11]. It's about providing the context a reader needs to understand why your work is interesting and how it's limited. I try to have at least one citation for each step in an important argument, eg why\n333\t\n334\tThe introduction is where you have room to define key terms and concepts required to understand your claims, especially if they're somewhat technical.\n335\t\n336\tIt's often good to explicitly end with a bullet-point list of your contributions, which are basically just the concise claims you believe to be true, potentially with brief references to the supporting evidence.\n337\t\n338\tFigures\n339\tFigures are incredibly important. Having clear graphs can be the difference between a very clear and easy-to-read paper and an incomprehensible mess.\n340\t\n341\tTo create a good figure:\n342\t\n343\tAsk yourself, \"What exactly is the information I would like someone to take away from this?\" It's not just about finding the list of numbers output by your experiments and shoving them into some standard plotting software, you want to carefully choose a visualisation that emphasises the desired information and takeaway.\n344\tAsk yourself, \"Why does this experiment tie back to my core claims? How would I like the reader to interpret these results? Which parts do I want to draw their attention to?\"\n345\tConsider annotating a graph or, if there's one particularly important line, emphasising it\n346\tE.g. make it dark while the others are light and low opacity, or all the other ones of the same color.\n347\tInclude standard elements like axis titles, a clear caption that explains what the figure is, how to interpret it, or at least where in the text they should look to understand what's going on.\n348\tMake sure the axis title and ticks are large enough to read, and have a good clear legend.\n349\tOften you can compress a fair amount of information into one graph - for example, using different sizes and shapes of markers on a scatterplot, different colors, etc.\n350\tFor heatmaps, if your data is positive and starts at zero, use a color scale that is white at zero and dark at the max (in plotly, \"blues\" is good). If your data is positive and negative with zero as a meaningful neutral point, use a color scale where zero is white (in plotly, \"RdBu\" is good).\n351\tAvoid having reds and greens conveying key information, 4% of people are red-green colourblind\n352\tIt can work well to combine several key graphs into one figure and make it your figure 1. E.g.:\n353\t\n354\tLanguage models represent space and time:\n355\t\n356\t\n357\t\n358\tNot all features are one-dimensionally linear:\n359\t\n360\t\n361\t\n362\tAnother kind of figure is an explanatory diagram rather than a graph. This can be a high-effort but very effective figure one, that gives people a sense of roughly what is happening in the paper. This should be something that would catch people's eye if you put it as the first image in a tweet thread about your paper. Some diagrams I liked (intentionally at several different levels of effortful):\n363\t\n364\tEmergent Misalignment:\n365\t\n366\t\n367\t\n368\tOn the Biology of a Large Language Model:\n369\t\n370\t\n371\t\n372\tCoT in the wild is not always faithful:\n373\t\n374\t\n375\t\n376\tRefusal is mediated by a single direction:\n377\t\n378\t\n379\t\n380\tMy grokking modular addition work:\n381\t\n382\t\n383\t\n384\tMain Body (Background, Methods and Results)\n385\tMost of the actual paper, by word count, should be about communicating your experiments and results in precise technical detail. To do good science, it is important that researchers can understand exactly what you did and what you observed, so they can draw their own conclusions rather than needing to take things on faith. For example, in interpretability, there are ways that a method can give completely useless answers if misapplied, so it’s crucial that I know if a paper did that, even though the detail might seem totally unimportant to the authors!\n386\t\n387\tIdeally, you want to communicate the information at several different layers of abstraction. It's your job to ensure that readers understand:\n388\t\n389\tThe key background context required to disambiguate and understand your work - key terms, techniques, etc[12][13]\n390\t\n391\tWhat your results are and how to interpret those results and their significance\n392\tWhat you actually did for your experiments\n393\tWhy this was reasonable/well motivated/relevant to your claims\n394\tThe specifics of various technical choices you made, and their implications for how to understand the results.\n395\tFor structure, here’s a good default:\n396\t\n397\tBackground: to explain the relevant context and terms - in particular, please define terminology and crucial techniques!\n398\tIf pressed for space, you can put a glossary of key terms/definitions as an appendix, I always appreciate this\n399\tIf you’re defining something new for this paper, put this in a section which is clearly not about reviewing known things (a new section or separate subsection)\n400\tMethods: Explain the methods you used and why they are relevant to the problem\n401\tResults: Specify exactly how the methods are applied as experiments, and what the results are\n402\tIf you have a bunch of experiments using fairly similar methods, put each in a different subsection\n403\tIf the experiments for each claim are more boutique, or if there are several claims with different styles of evidence, then I try to give each type of evidence its own section while explaining how it ties back to the overarching theme, rather than a methods -> results section. People will forget about the first method before they see its results.\n404\t\n405\tDiscussion\n406\tExplaining the limitations of your work is a crucial part of scientific good practice. The goal of a paper is to contribute to our body of knowledge. Readers must understand the limitations of the evidence you provide to have a calibrated sense of what knowledge they have learned. And it's important that you put a good faith effort into documenting limitations because you know far more about your work than the readers, so they may miss things.\n407\t\n408\tThere is a common mistake of trying to make your work sound maximally exciting. Generally, the people whose opinions you most care about are competent researchers who can see through this kind of thing. And I generally have a much higher opinion of a piece of work if it clearly acknowledges its limitations up front. I’m not sure if this makes it easier or harder to get published.\n409\t\n410\tThis is also the place to discuss broader implications and general takeaways, future work you’d be excited about, reflections, etc.\n411\t\n412\tSome people have conclusions too. Personally, I think conclusions are often kind of useless; the introduction should have explained this well. You can skip it\n413\t\n414\tRelated Work\n415\tGenerally, related work is often treated like a bit of an annoyance and afterthought. The feeling that you need to cite lots of things that aren't actually relevant can be annoying, but sometimes there is very important work that has done similar things to you, and a reader might have seen that and wonder why your paper is interesting.\n416\t\n417\tIt's very important to clearly explain why what you did is different or, if what you did is not very different, either acknowledge this (\"that was parallel work\") or explain why your work is still slightly interesting in this context, or how you fixed a mistake in prior work (stated politely).\n418\t\n419\tBut that said, there’s a lot of annoying norms here and related works often add little value IMO - needing to cite a lot so you look like you’ve put in enough effort, covering minor or obscure things that aren’t particularly relevant, citing low quality works to be polite, making sure to cite the first instance of each thing, etc. Contextualising in the literature is important, but ideally I’ve already covered it in the introduction.\n420\t\n421\tRelated work is often put as the second section of the paper. Personally, I generally prefer it to be the penultimate section. I think related work should only be upfront if it plays an important role in motivating the paper - if your paper is very heavily tied to the surrounding literature, plugging a gap, correcting a mistake, or unlocking a new capability that would enhance various bits of prior work.\n422\t\n423\tAppendices\n424\tAppendices are weird. They're basically the place you put everything that doesn't fit into the main paper. One way to think about it is that you're actually writing a much longer than nine-page paper - the main body and the appendices - but you've chosen a highlights reel for the first nine pages where you put all the absolutely key information. You place all the less crucial information in the appendices for readers to pick and choose from as they see fit.\n425\t\n426\tIn general, the crucial scarce resource you must manage is the reader's time and attention. The main body should be aggressively prioritized to make the most of this, be engaging, and communicate the most important pieces of information. But if you have a lot more to say than you can fit in there, then that's what appendices are for. A truly interested reader can go and take a look, though most won't.\n427\t\n428\tGenerally, appendices are held to a notably lower standard than the main body and will be read far less, so you should not feel obliged to put in meaningful effort polishing them. This is the standard solution to the dilemma when you want to include full technical detail but have done some fairly complex and convoluted work that just won't realistically fit.\n429\t\n430\tCommon Pitfalls and How to Avoid Them\n431\tObsessing Over Publishability\n432\tPeer review is notoriously terrible for seeking truth. Reviewers often have biases, like favoring work that feels novel and shiny and exciting, or that doesn't feel weird or too new, or that doesn’t seriously challenge their existing beliefs. This has been shown in rigorous RCTs, where NeurIPS 2021 gave some papers two sets of reviewers and compared their decisions. The results… aren’t great:\n433\t\n434\t\n435\t\n436\tI personally think that, at least in safety, doing good work that people respect matters more than getting into conferences, though both are nice. I’ve generally had fairly good results with just trying to write high-integrity work that explains why I believe it is interesting, and just trying to do good science and the work that I think is highest impact, even if it doesn't fit the academic mold.\n437\t\n438\tBut it’s pretty plausible to me that many of the people reading this are not in such a fortunate position, and that getting first author papers into top conferences would be a meaningful career boost, especially your first 1-2 papers. The strategy I generally recommend for my mentees is to spend most of the project doing the best scientific work they can. Then, as we approach the end of the project, we figure out how to wrap it up in a maximally conference-friendly package while writing and submitting it. If we did anything that made the work noticeably worse, we can undo it before uploading to Arxiv.\n439\t\n440\tUnnecessary Complexity and Verbosity\n441\tPapers are seen as prestigious, formal, and highly intellectual artifacts. As a result, there's a tendency towards verbosity or trying to make things sound more complex and fancy than they actually are, so they feel impressive. I think this is a highly ineffective strategy. If I don’t understand a paper, I generally ignore it and move on, or assume it’s BS in the absence of strong evidence to the contrary. Often, the best papers just take some very simple techniques and apply them carefully and well. There’s a real elegance to being simple and effective.\n442\t\n443\tPeople need to understand a paper in order to appreciate it and build on it and think it is interesting (except for superficial Twitter clickbait). Generally, you want to be precise, but within the constraint of being precise, be as simple and accessible as possible. Try to use plain language and minimize jargon except where the jargon is needed to precisely convey your meaning. You get points for quality technical insights, not for sounding fancy. Verbosity and overly complex language and jargon is actively detrimental to your paper’s prospects, IMO.\n444\t\n445\tNot Prioritizing the Writing Process\n446\tPeople often do not prioritize writing. They treat it like an annoying afterthought and do all the fun bits like running experiments, and leave it to the last minute. This is a mistake. Again, your work only matters if people read and understand it. Writing quality majorly affects clarity and engagement. Writing is absolutely crucial and is a major multiplier on the impact of your work.\n447\t\n448\tI typically recommend that people switch from understanding mode to distillation and paper writing a month before a conference deadline, if at all possible. You should want to spend a lot of your time iterating on a write-up, getting feedback, trying to make it clearer, thinking about weaknesses, etc.\n449\t\n450\tTacit Knowledge and Beyond\n451\tOne irritation I have about the standard paper structure is that it heavily incentivizes being rigorous and maximally objective and defensible. Obviously, there are significant advantages to this, but I think that often a lot of the most valuable insights from a research project come in the form of tacit knowledge.\n452\t\n453\tThis might be:\n454\t\n455\tThis was hard and here are the steps we had to follow to get it to work.\n456\tHere are some ways we noticed our experiments catching fire and what we did to fix them.\n457\tHere's my fuzzy intuition of what's going on in the big picture - I can't fully defend it, but I'm reasonably confident this is true after several months of screwing around in this domain.\n458\tHere’s something I misunderstood for months before it suddenly clicked\n459\tHere’s a common misconception in this domain, or way people often misunderstand or overreact to our results\n460\tHere’s my advice to anyone replicating this work, especially how to find hyper-parameters and deal with the fiddly bits\n461\tFleshing out a plan for future work directions you find particularly exciting.\n462\tI think this is really important, and I find it a real shame that this is often just discarded. I am personally a big fan of putting this kind of stuff as appendix A or as an accompanying blog post, where you can take as many liberties as you like.\n463\t\n464\tConclusion\n465\tYour research will only matter if people read it, understand it, engage with it, and ideally believe it. This means that good paper writing is a crucial skill, but often neglected.\n466\t\n467\tThe core process should be to find the concise claims you believe to be true, the strongest experimental evidence that you believe builds a robust case for these claims, and use this to craft a coherent narrative. Then flesh this out into a bullet point outline of the overall post, reflect on it, and ideally get feedback, and iteratively expand.\n468\t\n469\tAgain, this is a highly opinionated post about how I personally think about the process and philosophy of paper writing. I'm sure many researchers will strongly disagree with me on many important points, and the correct approach will vary significantly by field and norms.\n470\t\n471\t^\n472\t Note: I am in no way claiming that I follow this advice, especially in blog posts - this is my attempt to paint a platonic ideal, and advise on how to be a better person than I. Personally, I find actually writing academic papers pretty frustrating and much prefer blog posts\n473\t\n474\t^\n475\t I think writing great papers certainly helps, and if you’re new I recommend just trying to write the best paper you can, but there’s still a lot of depressingly perverse incentives from ML peer review\n476\t\n477\t^\n478\t I mention this purely for completeness. I have never seen a convincing guarantee in deep learning, neural networks are far too squishy\n479\t\n480\t^\n481\t OpenAI’s is also good, but Google’s is free!\n482\t\n483\t^\n484\t Well, at least “provide enough evidence to somewhat update their beliefs”, convince is a fairly high bar\n485\t\n486\t^\n487\t Which, admittedly, is fairly rare in ML as far as I’m aware\n488\t\n489\t^\n490\t Thanks to Paul Bogdan for these points on p-values\n491\t\n492\t^\n493\t Also the title, though I haven’t figured out how to productively spend 20% of my time on that yet…\n494\t\n495\t^\n496\t Paper swaps are a great way to get feedback - find someone else also working on a paper and offer to give each other feedback. Even if you have less time before the paper deadline, this tends to be a mutually beneficial trade.\n497\t\n498\t^\n499\t This can be its own paragraph, or part of paragraph 3\n500\t\n501\t^\n502\t Save that for the related work section…\n503\t\n504\t^\n505\t If something is super widespread knowledge, no need to cover it, but err towards defining things. E.g. I wouldn’t bother defining the transformer architecture or an LLM, but I would define a sparse autoencoder or steering vector\n506\t\n507\t^\n508\t If this is too long, you can move most of it to an appendix\n509\t\n510\t1.\n511\t Note: I am in no way claiming that I follow this advice, especially in blog posts - this is my attempt to paint a platonic ideal, and advise on how to be a better person than I. Personally, I find actually writing academic papers pretty frustrating and much prefer blog posts\n512\t\n513\t2.\n514\t I think writing great papers certainly helps, and if you’re new I recommend just trying to write the best paper you can, but there’s still a lot of depressingly perverse incentives from ML peer review\n515\t\n516\t3.\n517\t I mention this purely for completeness. I have never seen a convincing guarantee in deep learning, neural networks are far too squishy\n518\t\n519\t4.\n520\t OpenAI’s is also good, but Google’s is free!\n521\t\n522\t5.\n523\t Well, at least “provide enough evidence to somewhat update their beliefs”, convince is a fairly high bar\n524\t\n525\t6.\n526\t Which, admittedly, is fairly rare in ML as far as I’m aware\n527\t\n528\t7.\n529\t Thanks to Paul Bogdan for these points on p-values\n530\t\n531\t8.\n532\t Also the title, though I haven’t figured out how to productively spend 20% of my time on that yet…\n533\t\n534\t9.\n535\t Paper swaps are a great way to get feedback - find someone else also working on a paper and offer to give each other feedback. Even if you have less time before the paper deadline, this tends to be a mutually beneficial trade.\n536\t\n537\t10.\n538\t This can be its own paragraph, or part of paragraph 3\n539\t\n540\t11.\n541\t Save that for the related work section…\n542\t\n543\t12.\n544\t If something is super widespread knowledge, no need to cover it, but err towards defining things. E.g. I wouldn’t bother defining the transformer architecture or an LLM, but I would define a sparse autoencoder or steering vector\n545\t\n546\t13.\n547\t If this is too long, you can move most of it to an appendix\n548\t\n549\t"}]},"uuid":"3cee51b5-2f4e-4593-8cd4-411d870e8949","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:28.655Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4f1a1a7c-bcfc-4e98-8301-bd9eaad26d67","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"be11eba4-ac20-42d9-b37b-ee4fc1a9aeda","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PB8yZWQhPhZtJD4vqCwZAf","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Feature-Resolved Attention: when, why, and how much it beats linear steering — results summary\n2\t\n3\t*Compressed per the writing guidelines: the narrative, the claims, the evidence. Source material:\n4\t`THEORY.md`, `CAMPAIGN_REPORT.md`, `summary.md`; figures `fig1_final.png`, `fig6_retrieval.png`,\n5\t`fig9_lbnr_tier2.png`, `fig_sprint2_summary.png`, `fig_magnitude_law.png`.*\n6\t\n7\t## The one-paragraph narrative\n8\t\n9\tFeature-Resolved Attention (FRA) decomposes a head's pre-softmax attention score, in an SAE basis, into\n10\tan exact sum of **cells** `S[q,k] = Σ_{μν} u^μ_q u^ν_k ω_{μν}`, each a multiplicative AND of a\n11\tquery-content feature and a key-content feature. The corresponding **cell-level weight edit** (a\n12\tsupport-gated rank-1 update of W_QK) is a capability **no linear residual steer can express**: a steer\n13\tcan imitate a *row or column* of the bilinear form but never a *cell*, and it broadcasts into the\n14\tresidual stream where every consumer reads it. We turn this into a *predictive* and *quantitative*\n15\taccount of when FRA earns its keep as a behavioral-intervention tool, validate it across five model\n16\tbehaviors, and map the boundary of its (narrow) win-class.\n17\t\n18\t## The claims\n19\t\n20\t**Claim 1 (the win, measured against the strongest fair baseline).** For behaviors carried by a\n21\tcontent-query × content-key attention edge, an FRA cell-edit suppresses the behavior while leaving the\n22\tendpoints' *other* uses essentially untouched, at **10³–10⁴× lower collateral** than the strongest fair\n23\t*linear* baseline — a content-gated projection-removal steer — at matched on-target removal.\n24\t*Evidence:* legit-content KL at matched removal: retrieval (gemma-2-2b) **0.0016 vs 1.77 nats (~1100×)**;\n25\tacronym letter-movers (gpt2) **~0 vs 2.64 (~26,000×)**; copy-suppression on natural text **0.000 vs ~2.0,\n26\tA=516×**. Head-ablation and output-direction suppression (the baselines reported pre-red-team) are\n27\t*strawmen* — they break the behavior everywhere; the position-patch is fair but position-tied (fails\n28\tcontent-addressed transfer). The decisive, fair comparison is the linear steer, and FRA wins it by orders\n29\tof magnitude.\n30\t\n31\t**Claim 2 (the magnitude law).** The advantage obeys **A ≈ reuse(marginal endpoint) / reuse(conjunction)**,\n32\treuse measured on the evaluation distribution. *Evidence:* validated as a **quantitative curve** — in a\n33\tcontrolled sweep of N siblings sharing a value, generic pairs (conjunction recurs) give A flat at ~1\n34\t(1.4→2.0), while differential cells (conjunction made unique) give A growing ≈ N (6.3→13.4→23.8 over\n35\tN=2..4). This is the correct form of the magnitude predictor an earlier corpus-firing-rate attempt failed\n36\tto capture.\n37\t\n38\t**Claim 3 (the boundary — a predictive checklist, and a narrow win-class).** FRA gives a clean win iff:\n39\t(1) edge-routed (CCF-high, not positional/output-direction), (2) load-bearing & non-redundant (causal\n40\tedge-cut R≈1, no backups/alternative cues), (3) direct consumption (the behavior *is* the OV-transported\n41\tcontent, not downstream MLP), (4) conjunction-specific with a **distinctive-content discriminating query**\n42\t(a specific token/feature, not a generic role), (5) reach (SAE-explained × head-coverage × top-K clears\n43\tthe softmax margin). *Evidence:* confirmed wins — induction (15×), in-context backdoor (25×),\n44\tcopy-suppression (516×), retrieval (1100×), acronym (26,000×). Theory-predicted failures, each isolating\n45\tone clause — weight-baked sleeper (output direction, c1), greater-than (MLP-downstream, c3), IOI (backups,\n46\tc2), docstring/delimiter (redundant, c2), many-shot jailbreak (direction-routed), PII/entity retrieval\n47\t(positional/role query, c4), class-union (token-specific keys, c4). **Most established-benchmark behaviors\n48\tfall outside the win-class** — this is the central, honest finding: FRA is a *specialized* instrument, the\n49\t\"missing middle\" between direction steers (marginal + broadcast) and position patches (specific but not\n50\tcontent-addressed).\n51\t\n52\t## So what (why it matters)\n53\t\n54\t(i) A *principled, predictive* criterion for a bilinear interpretability primitive — you can decide\n55\tbefore you intervene. (ii) A demonstration that the bilinear (QK) structure of attention is **load-bearing**:\n56\tthere exist interventions a content-addressed *linear* steer provably cannot match. (iii) An honest map of\n57\twhere this helps — narrow, but real, and relevant to memorization/copy control, retrieval steering, and\n58\tin-context backdoor removal.\n59\t\n60\t## Methodological contributions (reusable)\n61\t- The **corrected Tier-2 metric**: legit-content KL vs a content-gated *projection-removal* linear steer\n62\t  at matched on-target removal (not A-ratio vs head-ablation strawmen). Sprint-1's red-team produced this.\n63\t- **Causal head-finding** (rank heads by edge-cut effect, not raw attention, which selects positional heads).\n64\t- **Differential cells** (target-edge minus sibling-edge) to recover target-specificity when siblings\n65\t  share an endpoint, at a quantified reach cost.\n66\t\n67\t## Relation to the prior result (→ appendix)\n68\tThe earlier write-up established the *first* FRA behavioral win — association-specific control of an\n69\tinduction edge, ~15× lower collateral than the fairest linear steer — and the sleeper-agent *bridge*\n70\t(weight-baked backdoor → DoM/SVD wins; in-context backdoor → FRA-QK wins), unifying removal-method choice\n71\tunder \"suppress what uniquely carries the behavior.\" This work subsumes that as one entry in the win-class,\n72\tadds the exact cell/set-algebra theory, the magnitude law, the corrected fair metric, and the boundary map.\n73\t"}]},"uuid":"adb216ca-8140-4a5b-b37d-2347efb28295","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:28.665Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"be11eba4-ac20-42d9-b37b-ee4fc1a9aeda","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"adb216ca-8140-4a5b-b37d-2347efb28295","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ada392f8670716699","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_017JLSe6ohSLsFJiS2c9LGbr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I'll continue reading the required files. Let me read the remaining source documents and the existing tex style file."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":813,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29173,"cache_read_input_tokens":12183,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":29173,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cbx7GeucZ5HP1wQ2zyjAr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"e6e4eab2-5f25-437c-84d3-d7f4eb542930","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:31.050Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"e6e4eab2-5f25-437c-84d3-d7f4eb542930","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ada392f8670716699","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_017JLSe6ohSLsFJiS2c9LGbr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018tsqNVbvSyUSe9gSLJYkCD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/THEORY.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":813,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29173,"cache_read_input_tokens":12183,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":29173,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cbx7GeucZ5HP1wQ2zyjAr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"93a3dc84-16ba-4154-b790-cde48e347edb","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:31.980Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"93a3dc84-16ba-4154-b790-cde48e347edb","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018tsqNVbvSyUSe9gSLJYkCD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# FRA Theory (Fable, sprint-2 — converged @ cycle 3)\n2\t\n3\t## Mechanistic theory\n4\tWHAT FRA UNIQUELY COMPUTES. A head h's pre-softmax score is a bilinear form on the post-norm residual: S^h[q,k] = (x_q W_Q^h)·(x_k W_K^h)/√d_h (row-vector convention; biases contribute pure row/column/constant terms). Substituting the SAE decomposition x_t = Σ_μ u^μ_t f_μ + e_t yields the EXACT partition S^h[q,k] = Σ_{μν} u^μ_q·u^ν_k·ω^h_{μν} + (feature×error) + (error×error) + bias terms, with ω^h_{μν} = (f_μ W_Q^h)·(f_ν W_K^h)/√d_h the weight-space pair coupling. Each (μ,ν) CELL is a multiplicative AND: its value u^μ_q·u^ν_k is identically zero unless the query-content feature AND the key-content feature both fire (u ≥ 0 under ReLU/JumpReLU). This is the only exact additive decomposition in the attention pathway that lives in the JOINT (query-content × key-content) function space: the OV side (C^OV = A·u^λ·⟨f_λ W_OV, d⟩) is merely linear-given-pattern, and the post-softmax pattern admits no canonical decomposition (the denominator couples all cells across all keys; attribution requires the Jacobian ∂A_qk/∂s_qj = A_qk(1_{k=j}−A_qj) and the centered OV profile g̃^h_{q,j} = A^h_{qj}(g^h_j−ḡ^h_q) — but the EDIT needs no linearization because it acts on s BEFORE the softmax). THE EDIT AND ITS EXPRESSIVE UNIQUENESS. The FRA edit subtracts c·u^μ_q·u^ν_k·ω^h_{μν} from one head's scores wherever the conjunction fires. With the SAE gates frozen it is exactly a support-gated rank-1 update of the head's bilinear form, ΔW^h_QK ∝ −c·ω_{μν}·e_μᵀe_ν restricted to {(q,k): u^μ_q>0 ∧ u^ν_k>0}; its only causal channel is that head's softmax→OV transport. FRA additionally supports SET ALGEBRA over cells: UNIONS across pairs/heads — including whole feature FAMILIES (class-level edits: cut every (attribute-query × value-class-key) cell at once) — and DIFFERENCES (differential cells = target-edge top-M minus sibling-edge top-M), which buy back target-specificity when siblings share an endpoint (measured: sibling separability 1.9×→7.9× at a quantified reach cost 58%→33%). A linear residual steer Δ (ActAdd/DoM/CAA/projection-removal), even gated by an ARBITRARY content gate g(x), is doubly weaker. (a) SCORE-SPACE EXPRESSIVITY: a gated query-side write changes scores by δs[q,k] = g(x_q)·(Δ W_Q^h)·(x_k W_K^h)ᵀ/√d, which varies over keys only through x_k's projection onto ONE fixed pullback direction v = Δ W_Q^h W_K^hᵀ. Cancelling a single cell requires ⟨x_k, v⟩ ∝ u^ν(x_k) for ALL k — impossible: u^ν is a thresholded nonlinearity of x_k, and even its linear part forces hitting the entire ν-ROW of the bilinear form plus every key cosine-close to v. A steer can imitate a row or a column of W_QK, never a cell — and a fortiori never a cell-difference or a family-union. (b) RESIDUAL BROADCAST: the write Δ is deposited into the stream and read by EVERY consumer at the gated positions — all heads' Q, K AND V, the MLP, every later layer. Gating restricts WHERE the steer fires, never WHAT reads it. These two facts ARE the measured separability gap: at matched on-target removal, a content-gated projection-removal steer strong enough to suppress the behavior destroys the content everywhere it legitimately appears (legit-content KL 1.77–2.64 nats), while the FRA cell-edit leaves it at ~0.000–0.0016 (≈1,000–26,000×), because legitimate occurrences fail the conjunction. THE MAGNITUDE LAW (validated at both extremes): A ≈ reuse(marginal endpoint)/reuse(conjunction), with reuse measured ON THE EVAL/DEPLOYMENT SUPPORT (naive corpus firing rates are degenerate — the s5 failure). A ~ 10³–10⁴ when the conjunction is unique (retrieval 1100×, acronym 26,000×); A → ~1.9× floor when a GENERIC endpoint makes the conjunction recur (shared-endpoint sibling test). SHARPENED COROLLARY (cycle 2): the discriminating endpoint — usually the query — must be distinctive CONTENT (a specific token/feature), NOT a generic ROLE feature. Positionally-resolved retrieval (box/entity identity) uses a generic \"queried-slot\" feature, so the (query × value) conjunction recurs across siblings and FRA cannot separate them (box A=7.9× partial only at weak base; entity-PII no-op at strong base). Every confirmed win has a content query: induction (query = token X), copy-suppression (query = about-to-predict-X), acronym (query = the spelled letters), in-context backdoor (query = the trigger content). NONLINEARITY LEDGER (what is exact where): RMSNorm is a frozen per-position SCALAR × diag(γ) — it commutes into the activations given the cached rms, so the score decomposition is exact on Gemma (edge-magnitude ratio 1.07 confirms). GPT-2 LayerNorm adds the fixed centering projector (I−11ᵀ/d); the 50–75% edge-reconstruction shortfall is a LN-folding-convention artifact, not an FRA property; ln1.hook_normalized is the gold-standard hookpoint but no public SAE suite ships it, and the grms check showed the hookpoint is not the limiter on RMSNorm models. RoPE is a per-position ROTATION applied AFTER projection: ω^h_{μν}(q−k) = ((f_μ W_Q^h)R_q)·((f_ν W_K^h)R_k)ᵀ/√d depends only on the relative offset (block structure R_qR_kᵀ = R_{q−k}) — still exact per edge; the edit stays content-addressed with an offset-dependent coefficient. GPT-2's learned absolute positional embeddings enter simply as residual content (positional features → the CCF sink-mask handles them). Gemma's soft-cap s ↦ 50·tanh(s/50) is the ONE true nonlinearity between the bilinear sum and the softmax: cells are exact pre-cap, and an edit Δs reaches the pattern attenuated by tanh′(s/50) ≤ 1, so edits are compressed exactly on saturated edges. This — plus the SAE-unexplained score fraction (the error×· terms) and granularity×top-K matching (65k GemmaScope is the sweet spot; 1M-width fragments an edge across too many tiny cells for a fixed pair budget) — is the complete account of FRA's suppression-reach ceiling. The ceiling bounds REACH, never separability: whatever FRA removes, it removes only on the conjunction's support. SUMMARY: FRA computes the exact joint query-content×key-content functional decomposition of attention selection, and hands you the corresponding cell-level (and cell-set-level) weight edit whose causal support is a content conjunction — the missing middle between direction steers (marginal + broadcast to all readers) and position patches (specific but not content-addressed).\n5\t\n6\t## Win checklist\n7\t1. EDGE-ROUTED (CCF-high): the behavior's proximate cause is pre-softmax score mass on identifiable attention edges, dominated by non-sink content×content feature pairs after sink-masking (CCF reliable on gpt2-small; on gemma the current sink-mask over-kills late layers, so certify behaviorally via LBNR). Kills positional/BOS edges (CCF=0.000) and output-direction behaviors with no score-space cause (weight-baked sleeper).\n8\t2. LOAD-BEARING & NON-REDUNDANT (LBNR-R ≈ 1, measured BEHAVIORALLY with CAUSAL head-finding): cutting the identified feature-pair edge across ALL heads that carry it (heads ranked by causal edge-cut effect, never raw attention — raw attention selects positional/sink heads) removes the downstream behavior, and no backup circuit OR alternative cue restores it. Kills IOI (R=−0.39, backups overcompensate), docstring (R=0.08), delimiter matching (R=0.23, grammar provides redundant cues), gemma knowledge-conflict (R=0.24).\n9\t3. DIRECT CONSUMPTION: the behavior IS the edge's OV-transported content (the attended token's write into the output), not a downstream MLP computation that merely reads the attended information — so LBNR must be evaluated on the behavioral metric, never on attention or an intermediate. Kills greater-than (the > is MLP-computed; edge-cut dents behavior only 0.98→0.87).\n10\t4. CONJUNCTION-SPECIFIC ON THE EVAL DISTRIBUTION, with a DISTINCTIVE-CONTENT discriminating endpoint: at least one endpoint feature legitimately recurs in deployment (so every direction/content-gated steer pays collateral), while the specific conjunction does NOT recur legitimately — which requires the discriminating endpoint (usually the query) to be distinctive CONTENT (a specific token/phrase/letter-state feature), not a generic ROLE feature; positionally-resolved identity (box/entity slots) fails this clause by construction. If siblings share the value endpoint, differential cells (target-edge minus sibling-edge) must retain enough non-shared score mass; if the goal is class-level removal, the UNION of the family's cells must be enumerable. Kills rare-dedicated-token triggers (a direction is already surgical, A→1) and generic-role-query retrieval (sibling A=1.9×; entity-PII no-op).\n11\t5. REACH (bounds removal completeness, not the win/lose verdict): SAE-explained fraction of the edge score × causal head coverage × top-K pair budget — with SAE granularity matched to K (65k GemmaScope sweet spot; 1M fragments at fixed K) — × soft-cap headroom tanh′(s/50) must clear the softmax margin on every carrying head at faithful scale c≈1–2, so that a matched-removal operating point EXISTS at which the separability comparison is defined.\n12\t\n13\t**Underlying reason:** The whole checklist is one separability statement. The behavior's minimal sufficient cause must be a SEPARABLE PRODUCT TERM u^μ_q·u^ν_k inside one (or an enumerable few) heads' score functions, whose support is disjoint from legitimate use of its endpoints ON THE EVAL DISTRIBUTION. When it is: (i) the FRA edit's support is exactly the conjunction's support and its only causal channel is those heads' softmaxes, so its collateral scales with reuse(conjunction|eval) — ~0 when the pairing is the targeted/malicious thing; (ii) ANY linear residual steer, however gated, must either suppress a marginal endpoint — its score effect is confined to a row/column of the bilinear form (one fixed pullback direction over keys) — or write into the residual stream where all heads' Q/K/V, the MLP, and every later layer read it; so its collateral scales with reuse(marginal|eval) × (number of residual readers). The win ratio at matched removal is therefore A ≈ reuse(marginal|eval)/reuse(conjunction|eval), validated at both extremes: ~10³–10⁴ when the conjunction is unique (retrieval 1100×, acronym 26,000×) and a ~1.9× floor when a generic role endpoint makes the conjunction recur (shared-endpoint sibling), with differential cells buying specificity back (7.9×) at a quantified reach cost (58%→33%). CCF certifies the cause lives in score space; LBNR certifies the conjunction is the unique carrier (against both backup circuits and redundant distributional cues); direct-consumption certifies the product term's output IS the behavior; conjunction-specificity-with-content-discriminator certifies the ratio's denominator is small and its numerator large; reach certifies the basis can express enough of the term to act on. Each failure mode is the negation of exactly one factor.\n14\t\n15\t**Failure modes:**\n16\t- OUTPUT-DIRECTION ROUTING (weight-baked sleeper, emergent misalignment): the payload is written through OV/unembedding as a residual DIRECTION with near-zero legitimate reuse; there is no score-space cause to edit, and DoM/SVD on the marginal direction is already surgical — no bilinear gap exists (negates edge-routing and the reuse gap).\n17\t- REDUNDANT / SELF-REPAIRING CIRCUITS (IOI name-movers R=−0.39 with backup attention 0.34→0.69; docstring R=0.08): cutting the edge triggers backup heads that restore — or overshoot — the behavior; the conjunction is not the unique carrier, so the edit is precise but behaviorally inert (negates LBNR).\n18\t- DISTRIBUTIONAL REDUNDANCY OF STRUCTURAL PREDICTION (delimiter/quote-type matching, CCF=0.732 but R=+0.23 — the cycle-2 negative class): the edge is a genuine content×content conjunction, but the predicted output is ALSO derivable from many other cues the distribution provides (grammar gives many ways to know a paren must close), so the edge is not load-bearing even with no dedicated backup circuit (negates LBNR by cue-redundancy rather than head-redundancy).\n19\t- DISTRIBUTED / DIRECTION-ROUTED IN-CONTEXT BEHAVIOR (many-shot jailbreak; binding R=−0.89; gemma knowledge-conflict R=0.24): the behavior rides a distributed ICL task-direction across many weak edges, not a separable pair; worse, the injected behavior can be representationally identical to a legitimate one ('jailbroken enthusiasm' = 'genuine enthusiasm'), so NO method removes it cleanly — there is no separable link to cut (negates edge-routing and LBNR).\n20\t- DOWNSTREAM NONLINEAR CONSUMPTION (greater-than): the QK edge is real and content-conjunctive, but an MLP computes the behavior from the attended information; cutting the edge controls the attending, not the computation (negates direct consumption).\n21\t- CONJUNCTION RECURRENCE — generic role endpoint, positional identity, or collision (shared-endpoint sibling A=1.9×; entity-PII differential no-op at strong base; acronym 'Unit' duplicate): the target conjunction's support overlaps legitimate instances of the SAME pair, either literally (duplicate target word) or because the discriminating endpoint is a generic role feature ('box-query', 'entity-slot') so identity is resolved POSITIONALLY, not by content — FRA's content-addressing correctly fires on the siblings too; collateral by design, A collapses toward 1. Partial rescue: differential cells (1.9→7.9×) at reach cost, IF non-shared mass exists (negates conjunction-specificity).\n22\t- REACH CEILING (Gemma backdoor removal 1/4→3/4 at 16k→65k; 1M-width regression at fixed K; GPT-2 LN-centering 50–75% reconstruction; soft-cap compression tanh′(s/50) on saturated edges; uncovered carrying heads): the SAE basis is too coarse, too fine for the pair budget, the folding drops centering, the cap eats the edit, or a carrying head is missed — FRA cannot hit the matched-removal operating point, so the win is capped or undefined even though separability holds for what it can remove (negates reach).\n23\t- POSITIONAL / SINK EDGE (BOS: raw attention 0.98 but CCF=0.000; lost-in-the-middle): the edge is selected by position, not content; a position-patch is the correct tool and content-addressing adds nothing (negates the content clause of edge-routing)."}]},"uuid":"6b9a7cee-179c-40ed-9c6e-4f3e2ff5008a","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:31.995Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"93a3dc84-16ba-4154-b790-cde48e347edb","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"6b9a7cee-179c-40ed-9c6e-4f3e2ff5008a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ada392f8670716699","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_017JLSe6ohSLsFJiS2c9LGbr","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TTDUPfYTHYKBFE89Xh9qqz","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/CAMPAIGN_REPORT.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":813,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29173,"cache_read_input_tokens":12183,"output_tokens":226,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":29173},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":813,"output_tokens":226,"cache_read_input_tokens":12183,"cache_creation_input_tokens":29173,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":29173,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cbx7GeucZ5HP1wQ2zyjAr","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"1e007486-8b9e-48e0-8c37-a5d297a15191","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:32.338Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"1e007486-8b9e-48e0-8c37-a5d297a15191","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TTDUPfYTHYKBFE89Xh9qqz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# FRA candidate campaign — brainstorm → rank → screen → Tier-2 → red-team\n2\t\n3\t*Autonomous multi-agent run, 2026-06-10. Goal: find PUBLISHED behaviors with established benchmarks\n4\twhere FRA-QK gives a real behavioral-intervention advantage, ranked by the validated CCF∧LBNR predictor,\n5\tevaluated, and adversarially red-teamed. Pipeline: 2 Fable + 6 Opus brainstorm agents → Opus ranking →\n6\tGPU evaluation (pod) → 4 Opus red-team agents + synthesis. Log: `CAMPAIGN_LOG.md`.*\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## TL;DR\n11\t\n12\t- **The screen works as a predictor.** Of 21 brainstormed candidates, the ranker shortlisted 5; the\n13\t  CCF∧LBNR screen + Tier-2 correctly separated a new pass (acronym letter-movers) from the screen-outs\n14\t  (docstring, fact-recall) and from the 13 it killed a priori (direction-routed / MLP / redundant).\n15\t- **One new candidate passed the screen and the Tier-2 metric-validity gauntlet: acronym letter-movers**\n16\t  (Garcia-Carrasco et al. 2024, gpt2-small). CCF 0.886, LBNR R=0.95; pair-specific (random-pair null\n17\t  clean); scale-robust; A *grows* to ~58× at matched on-target removal.\n18\t- **The red-team correctly DOWNGRADED the *headline* — but the decisive follow-up then CONFIRMED the win.**\n19\t  \"A=38× vs head-ablation\" is the wrong metric (non-selective strawman; a position-patch ties it on the\n20\t  cross-acronym panel). The RIGHT metric is **legit-content KL vs a content-gated linear steer** (the\n21\t  strongest fair baseline). With a *working* steer (§4, §7), **FRA beats it by 4 orders of magnitude on\n22\t  both wins**: a content-gated steer strong enough to suppress the behavior *destroys the content\n23\t  everywhere it appears* (acronym legit-Officer KL 2.64, retrieval legit-frog KL 1.77), while FRA cuts\n24\t  only the query×key **pair** and preserves it (KL ~0.000 / 0.0016). **The bilinear-QK structure IS\n25\t  load-bearing** — FRA does what no content-addressed *linear* steer can.\n26\t- **Retrieval is the strongest confirmed win** (§7): clean pair-specificity (random off-edge null does\n27\t  nothing), real separability, transfer beats position-patch, and **~1100× more separable than the\n28\t  content-gated steer**. Acronym confirms the same (~26,000×).\n29\t- **Process lesson (campaign-level, the main deliverable):** the standard fair Tier-2 baseline is a\n30\t  **content-gated linear steer (projection-removal)** compared on **legit-content KL at matched on-target\n31\t  removal** — not head-ablation/content-suppress at default strength. Under this corrected metric the FRA\n32\t  wins are *stronger and cleaner* than the original A-ratios suggested.\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## 1. Brainstorm + rank (workflow ws1u06t61, 2 Fable + 6 Opus → Opus rank)\n37\t\n38\t21 candidates over published behavior families (retrieval heads, backtracking, sleepers, EM, refusal,\n39\tfunction vectors, successor/entity-tracking, wildcard). Ranked shortlist:\n40\t\n41\t| # | candidate | paper / benchmark | predicted | feasible |\n42\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n43\t| 1 | docstring next-arg retrieval | Heimersheim & Janiak 2023 / ACDC docstring task | win | gemma-2-2b |\n44\t| 2 | **acronym next-letter** | Garcia-Carrasco 2024 / 800-acronym set | uncertain (CCF open) | gpt2-small ✓ |\n45\t| 3 | scoped retrieval (bridge) | Wu et al. 2024 / NIAH-style | win (redundant) | gemma-2-2b |\n46\t| 4 | fact-recall | Geva 2023 / CounterFact | uncertain (MLP-risk) | gemma-2-2b |\n47\t| 5 | backtracking error-localization | Ward/Nanda | uncertain | gemma-2-2b-it |\n48\t\n49\t**Killed a priori (ranks 9–18), all correctly per the principle:** refusal & harmful-detection,\n50\tsuccessor heads, entity-tracking, emergent-misalignment, function vectors, the real Wu NIAH benchmark\n51\t(infeasible: smallest model Yi-6B has no SAEs), backtracking *onset*, weight-baked sleeper, many-shot\n52\tjailbreak — all direction-routed, MLP-downstream, redundant, or infeasible.\n53\t\n54\t## 2. Screen results (CCF + LBNR on the pod)\n55\t\n56\t| candidate | model | CCF | LBNR-R | verdict |\n57\t|---|---|---:|---:|---|\n58\t| **acronym** (heads 8.11/9.9/10.10/11.4) | gpt2 | **0.886** | **+0.95** (P(O) 0.62→0.03) | **PASS both** |\n59\t| retrieval-bridge (sanity) | gemma | n/a* | +0.63 | known win, re-confirmed |\n60\t| docstring | gemma | n/a* | **+0.08** | FAIL LBNR (redundant: P(files) 0.98→0.90) |\n61\t| fact-recall | gemma | n/a* | +0.86 | load-bearing edge but behavior too weak (base P=0.039) |\n62\t\n63\t*Methodological note: `g_screen`'s CCF read 0.000 for **all** gemma edges including the known retrieval\n64\twin — its sink-mask is over-aggressive on gemma's late-layer heads (the careful r5 setup gives retrieval\n65\tCCF=0.194). gemma verdicts therefore rest on LBNR (behavioral, reliable); gpt2 CCF is reliable. Also\n66\tfixed a head-selection bug: ranking heads by *raw attention* picks positional/sink heads — must rank by\n67\t*causal* edge-cut (cut → behavior drops).\n68\t\n69\t## 3. Acronym Tier-2 + red-team\n70\t\n71\t**Tier-2 (as run):** FRA ablates the (acronym-query × Officer-key) pairs. On-target P(O) 0.617→0.011;\n72\tcollateral on 3 other acronyms FRA 0.008 vs head-ablation 0.322 vs content-suppress 0.341 → A=38×/40×;\n73\ttransfer (proper, Officer at pos 4 vs 11): FRA 0.609→0.050 vs position-patch 0.609→0.608.\n74\t\n75\t**Red-team verdict: `downgrade-to-existence-proof`.**\n76\t- **SURVIVES — metric validity** (an agent re-ran on GPU, `acronym_redteam_matched.json`): random-pair\n77\t  null clean (random pairs at c=8 → 0.622 ≈ base, selected → 0.011); at matched removal (c=4: FRA 94.7%\n78\t  vs head-ablate 93.0%) A *grows* to 57.7× (collateral rises with c while removal saturates ~0.011, so\n79\t  c=8 was conservative); A≥35× across c∈[1,32]. **Pair-specific and scale-robust.**\n80\t- **WOUNDED (fatal to the headline) — baseline fairness:** A=38× is vs non-selective strawmen. The fair\n81\t  position-patch ties FRA on the cross-acronym metric (it fires on no other prompt either). FRA's real\n82\t  edge is transfer; the strongest fair baseline — a **content-gated linear steer** (content-addressed\n83\t  AND transfer-capable) — was the decisive missing experiment.\n84\t- **Honest caveats:** head-ablation collateral (0.322) is WWW→W-dominated (0.94), so the precise\n85\t  multiplier is fragile (order-of-magnitude robust); separability untested (legit base P(O)=0.000);\n86\t  external validity N=1–12; head 11.4 is causally ~dead, 10.6 is a real mover outside the named 4.\n87\t\n88\t## 4. The decisive experiment: FRA-QK vs content-gated linear steer\n89\t\n90\t**Result: INCONCLUSIVE (the steer baseline failed to fire) — this is now the #1 open experiment.**\n91\tThe content-gated steer I built (remove the dominant Officer-**key** SAE feature, content-gated,\n92\tscales a∈[0.5,8]) **did not suppress the letter-copy at all** (on-target P(O) 0.617→0.620), so there is\n93\tno matched-removal operating point to compare collateral at. On transfer (Officer at a new position):\n94\tFRA 0.609→**0.062** (suppresses), content-gated steer 0.610 (no effect, broken), position-patch 0.608\n95\t(fails, position-tied). KL-collateral on legit 'Officer' contexts was 0.000 for FRA (it correctly does\n96\tnot fire) — but also 0.000 for the steer (because the steer did nothing), so the discriminator is void.\n97\t\n98\t*First attempt (SAE-feature-subtraction) failed to fire (non-firing on gpt2, nan on gemma).* **Re-run\n99\twith a working PROJECTION-REMOVAL steer (`acronym_steer3`) RESOLVES it in FRA's favor:** the steer now\n100\tfires (a=4 → P(O) 0.001, matching FRA's 0.023), and on the decisive metric — **KL on legit 'Officer'\n101\tsentences** (acronym-query absent) — **FRA ~0.0000 vs content-gated steer 2.636 nats → FRA is ~26,000×\n102\tmore separable.** A content-gated linear steer strong enough to suppress the acronym **destroys the\n103\t'Officer' representation everywhere it appears**; FRA cuts only the (acronym-query × Officer-key) pair,\n104\tso it leaves legit Officer untouched. **Verdict: bilinear-QK is load-bearing — acronym is REHABILITATED\n105\ton the decisive axis.** (The original \"A=38× vs head-ablation\" headline is still the wrong metric — that\n106\tcollateral panel is tied by a position-patch; the RIGHT metric is legit-content KL vs the content-gated\n107\tsteer, where FRA wins by 4 orders of magnitude.)\n108\t\n109\t## 5. Distribution (external validity, `t_acronym_batch`)\n110\t\n111\tAcross 12 acronyms (6 with base P>0.15): median LBNR-R 0.93, median on-target removal 0.94, median A 10×\n112\t[2–59×] vs head-ablation. **A=38× (Officer) is the favorable end.** Confounds the red-team flagged: a\n113\tduplicate target word (\"Unit\" in CPU+GPU — content-addressing correctly hits both, wrongly counted as\n114\tcollateral, A=2×) and a non-load-bearing case (WWW, R=0.01 → A invalid). A clean external-validity result\n115\tneeds the public 800-acronym dataset, unique target words, LBNR-filtering, and single/multi-token\n116\tstratification.\n117\t\n118\t## 6. Verdict & what the campaign produced\n119\t\n120\t1. **The CCF∧LBNR predictor fired correctly** (acronym CCF 0.886 / R 0.95) and the kill-list (ranks\n121\t   9–18) holds — the screen is validated as a *triage* tool on a fresh candidate set.\n122\t2. **Acronym letter-movers is a real screen-predicted existence proof** of pair-specific, scale-robust,\n123\t   content-addressed attention control on a published benchmark — but **not** a \"38× win\"; the defensible\n124\t   claim is content-addressed transfer, pending the content-gated-steer test (§4).\n125\t3. **The headline-metric process fix** (matched removal × strongest fair selective baseline) applies\n126\t   retroactively to the whole FRA-win program, including the prior copy-suppression/retrieval wins —\n127\t   those should be re-reported the same way.\n128\t\n129\t---\n130\t\n131\t## 7. Retrieval red-team (the gauntlet applied to the prior r2 win) — **CONFIRMED**\n132\t\n133\tSame scrutiny that downgraded acronym, applied to the gemma-2-2b associative-retrieval win\n134\t(`retrieval_redteam.py`, `retrieval_steer2/3.py`). Unlike acronym, retrieval **survives every prong**,\n135\tincluding the decisive one acronym never resolved:\n136\t\n137\t| control | result | verdict |\n138\t|---|---|---|\n139\t| **clean random-null** (off-edge features, c=8) | selected pairs 0.021 vs random-off-edge **0.213 = base** (no effect) | **pair-specific** ✓ |\n140\t| **matched removal** vs head-ablation (both ~0.93 removal) | legit-frog KL FRA **0.002** vs head-ablate 0.010 | 5× more separable ✓ |\n141\t| **transfer** (frog at new position) | FRA 0.261→**0.044** vs position-patch 0.261→**0.261** (fails) | content-addressed ✓ |\n142\t| **DECISIVE: vs content-gated linear steer** (the strongest fair baseline) | at matched on-target removal (FRA 0.042 / steer 0.024), legit-frog KL FRA **0.0016** vs steer **1.77** | **FRA ~1100× more separable** ✓✓ |\n143\t\n144\t**The decisive result:** a content-gated linear steer (projection-removal of the frog-value direction)\n145\tis content-addressed and *does* suppress retrieval — but it **destroys the frog representation\n146\teverywhere frog-content appears** (legit-frog KL 1.77 nats). FRA cuts only the (box-query × frog-value)\n147\t**pair**, so it preserves legit frog (KL 0.0016) — **~1100× lower collateral than the strongest fair\n148\tbaseline at matched removal**. This is precisely the bilinear-QK advantage that acronym's broken steer\n149\tleft open: **the win is attributable to the QK pair structure, not to content-addressing per se.**\n150\t\n151\tNotes: the content-gated steer needed projection-removal (fp16-safe), not SAE-feature-subtraction\n152\t(which nan'd on gemma / didn't fire on gpt2 — the recurring obstacle); at a=2 it over-removes (flips to\n153\t0.58) and a≥4 nan's, so a=1 is the clean operating point. **Retrieval is the campaign's strongest\n154\tconfirmed FRA-QK win** — it has a genuine separability story (which acronym lacked) and it beats the\n155\tstrongest fair selective baseline by 3 orders of magnitude. *(Acronym re-tested with the same working\n156\tsteer — see `acronym_steer3.json`.)*\n157\t\n158\t**Net:** the same projection-removal steer is now the standard fair baseline for the whole program;\n159\tretrieval passes it decisively, which retroactively strengthens the r2 win to \"bilinear-QK confirmed.\"\n160\t\n161\t---\n162\t\n163\t# SPRINT 2 (theory-driven). Cycle 1.\n164\t\n165\t**Theory agent (Fable) — key contributions** (full: `THEORY.md`):\n166\t- FRA edits a **CELL** of the bilinear score form: S[q,k]=Σ_{μν} u^μ_q·u^ν_k·ω_{μν}; the edit's support is\n167\t  exactly the conjunction {q: u^μ_q>0} × {k: u^ν_k>0} and its only causal channel is one head's softmax.\n168\t- A linear residual steer (even content-gated) is doubly weaker: in score space it can imitate a **row or\n169\t  column** of W_QK but never a cell; in residual space its write is read by **every** consumer (all heads'\n170\t  Q/K/V, MLP, downstream) at the gated positions — gating restricts WHERE it fires, not WHAT reads it.\n171\t- **Magnitude law: A ≈ reuse(marginal endpoint) / reuse(conjunction)** — huge when both endpoints are\n172\t  common content but their pairing is rare/targeted; → 1 when the conjunction itself recurs.\n173\t- Nonlinearity accounting: RMSNorm = frozen per-position scalar (exact); RoPE = per-position rotation\n174\t  (exact per edge, relative-offset coupling); Gemma soft-cap is the one true nonlinearity (compresses\n175\t  edits on saturated edges) → bounds REACH, never the separability ratio.\n176\t\n177\t**Cycle-1 eval — the magnitude law is VALIDATED (a real theory result):**\n178\t- **Shared-endpoint sibling test** (`shared_endpoint_t2`): two boxes hold frog (red→frog, blue→frog);\n179\t  cut red's edge, measure blue. FRA suppresses red (0.199→0.083) but **also halves blue (0.095→0.046)** →\n180\t  sibling separability **A=1.9×** (vs the steer's blue 0.000). The retrieval head's **query feature is\n181\t  GENERIC** (\"box-query\"), so the (query × frog-value) conjunction **recurs** for blue →\n182\t  A ≈ reuse(frog)/reuse(conj) ≈ 2/2 ≈ 1. **This confirms the theory's conjunction-recurs bound and the\n183\t  magnitude law quantitatively.** (Differential-pair refinement — red-specific pairs = red-edge minus\n184\t  blue-edge — running to test whether FRA *can* be made target-specific.)\n185\t- Screened out: poison-RAG (gemma-base too weak, P=0.027), knowledge-conflict (R=0.24, distributed).\n186\t\n187\t**Implication for cycle 2:** clean wins need the **query feature to be TARGET-SPECIFIC** (not a generic\n188\trole feature) so the conjunction does NOT recur — i.e., the target must differ in *content the query head\n189\treads*, not just in a sibling that shares the value. The brainstorm should prioritize behaviors where the\n190\tquery-content is the discriminator (e.g., a rare/specific trigger-query × common-value), and avoid\n191\tshared-endpoint setups where a generic role-query makes the conjunction recur.\n192\t\n193\t**Cycle-1 differential-pair refinement (a methodological contribution):** the generic-pair failure\n194\t(A=1.9×) is FIXABLE by selecting **DIFFERENTIAL pairs** = target-edge top-M MINUS sibling-edge top-M\n195\t(red-specific = in red's edge, not blue's; 8–18 of 30 per head). Result: FRA suppresses red (0.199→0.134,\n196\tpartial) while **preserving blue (0.095→0.083, Δ0.012)** → sibling separability **A=7.9×** (vs the\n197\tcontent-gated steer which destroys blue, 0.000). **FRA CAN be made target-specific even when the target\n198\tshares its value with a sibling** — by isolating the pairs that distinguish the target's query/key from\n199\tthe sibling's. Trade-off (theory-predicted REACH bound): the red-specific subset has lower on-target reach\n200\t(33% vs the full-pair 58%), because selectivity costs pairs. **Method for cycle 2+: when a candidate has\n201\tsiblings sharing the value, select differential (target-minus-sibling) pairs; report A at matched removal\n202\twhere reach allows.**\n203\t\n204\t## SPRINT 2 — Cycle 2.\n205\t\n206\t**Theory refined** (`THEORY.md`): FRA supports **set algebra over cells** (unions across heads + DIFFERENCES = differential cells) — no rank-constrained linear residual object can express a cell, let alone a cell-difference. Magnitude law A≈reuse(marginal)/reuse(conjunction) measured on the **eval-distribution support** (the s5 corpus-firing-rate operationalization is degenerate). Clause-4 sharpened.\n207\t\n208\t**Cycle-2 evals — two theory-sharpening NEGATIVES:**\n209\t- **Delimiter/quote-type matching** (gpt2, paren): CCF=0.732 (a real content×content conjunction) but **LBNR R=+0.23 → FAIL**. Structural/syntactic prediction is **distributionally redundant** (grammar provides many cues to close a paren, not just the matching-opener edge). New negative class: CCF-high, LBNR-fail-by-redundancy.\n210\t- **PII / entity-attribute sibling** (gemma, Alice/Bob both→frog): differential (Alice-specific) pairs **do not suppress** Alice's strong retrieval (0.756→0.757) → the \"A=16.4×\" is **spurious** (zero on-target effect; matched-removal guard caught it). **FAIL.**\n211\t\n212\t**SHARPENED CLAUSE-4 (the cycle-2 theoretical yield):** FRA target-specificity requires the\n213\t*discriminating endpoint* (usually the query) to be **distinctive CONTENT** (a specific token/feature),\n214\tNOT a generic ROLE feature. Entity/box retrieval resolves identity **positionally** (the head uses a\n215\tgeneric \"the-queried-slot's-value\" query feature), so the (query × value) conjunction **recurs** across\n216\tsiblings and FRA cannot separate them — confirmed twice (box A→7.9× partial only at weak base; entity\n217\tno-op at strong base). The confirmed WINS all have content queries: induction (query = token X),\n218\tcopy-suppression (query = about-to-predict-X), acronym (query = the spelled letters). **Search\n219\timplication for cycle 3: prioritize behaviors whose QUERY is itself a distinctive content token, and\n220\tDEPRIORITIZE positionally-resolved retrieval / role-queries.**\n221\t\n222\t## SPRINT 2 — Cycle 3 (theory converged → eval-focused).\n223\t\n224\t**Theory converged** (`THEORY.md`): adds family-UNION edits (cut every (attribute-query × value-class-key)\n225\tcell at once) to the set-algebra; otherwise stable. Win-checklist now 5 clauses (edge-routed, LBNR,\n226\tdirect-consumption, conjunction-specific-with-distinctive-content-query, reach).\n227\t\n228\t**Class-UNION test (the distinctive untested capability) — NEGATIVE, but sharpening:** cutting the\n229\tfamily-union of (query × digit-class-key) cells did NOT suppress digit retrieval (red→7: 0.569→0.606).\n230\tThe digit-class feature exists and the union is expressible, but **retrieval routes through token-specific\n231\tkeys** (the \"7\"-feature), not the abstract class feature, so the class-level edit misses the load-bearing\n232\tcell. The linear digit-class steer broadcast catastrophically (held-out KL 9.7 vs FRA-union 0.000) —\n233\treconfirming the steer pathology, but FRA-union also did nothing. **Family-union is useful only if the\n234\tbehavior routes through the class feature; token-copy does not.**\n235\t\n236\t**THE BOUNDARY IS MAPPED (cycles 2–3, every extension a theory-predicted negative):**\n237\t| extension attempt | result | theory-predicted reason |\n238\t|---|---|---|\n239\t| delimiter/quote-type matching | FAIL (LBNR R=0.23) | structural prediction is distributionally redundant |\n240\t| PII / entity-attribute sibling | FAIL (no on-target effect) | entity-identity is positional; query feature is a generic role |\n241\t| class-level union (digit PII) | FAIL (union misses key) | retrieval routes through token-specific keys, not the class feature |\n242\t\n243\t**FRA's win-class is NARROW and now precisely characterized:** content-query, token-specific, conjunctive,\n244\tload-bearing, non-redundant attention edges (induction, copy-suppression, acronym, retrieval, in-context\n245\tbackdoor). Most established-benchmark behaviors fall OUTSIDE it (MLP-routed, positional, distributed,\n246\tredundant) — which is itself the campaign's central, honest finding.\n247\t\n248\t**Copy-suppression release on NATURAL text (win-side anchor) — clean positive:** on 8 varied natural\n249\tprompts, FRA selectively releases one token's copy-suppression (wolf: 16.57→17.76 ≈ edge-cut oracle)\n250\tat **A = 516×** less collateral than head-ablation (FRA 0.000 vs 0.517), and transfers to a new context\n251\t(16.34→18.10). Confirms clause-4 (content query \"about-to-predict-X\" → win) generalizes beyond the\n252\tsynthetic probe. (5/8 prompts show strong copy-suppression; 3 are weakly suppressed — the effect is\n253\ttoken-dependent, as expected.)\n254\t\n255\t---\n256\t\n257\t# SPRINT 2 SYNTHESIS — what FRA is, when it wins, and why\n258\t\n259\t*(`fig_sprint2_summary.png`; full theory in `THEORY.md`.)*\n260\t\n261\t## 1. What FRA uniquely computes (mechanism)\n262\tAn attention head's pre-softmax score is an exact bilinear form on the residual; in an SAE basis it\n263\tpartitions into **cells** `S[q,k]=Σ_{μν} u^μ_q·u^ν_k·ω_{μν}`, each a multiplicative AND of a query-content\n264\tfeature and a key-content feature. FRA hands you the **cell-level weight edit** (a support-gated rank-1\n265\tW_QK update) whose causal support is exactly the conjunction `{q: u^μ_q>0} × {k: u^ν_k>0}`. It also\n266\tsupports **set algebra over cells** — unions (incl. feature families) and **differences** (differential\n267\tcells). RMSNorm (per-position scalar) and RoPE (per-position rotation) keep this exact; Gemma's soft-cap\n268\tis the only true nonlinearity and bounds reach, not separability.\n269\t\n270\t## 2. Why no linear steer can match it (the core argument, now measured)\n271\tA content-gated linear steer is doubly weaker: (a) in **score space** it can imitate a *row or column* of\n272\tW_QK but never a *cell* (let alone a cell-difference or family-union); (b) in **residual space** its write\n273\tis read by *every* consumer (all heads' Q/K/V, MLP, every later layer) — gating restricts where it fires,\n274\tnever what reads it. **Measured:** at matched on-target removal, a steer strong enough to suppress the\n275\tbehavior destroys the content everywhere it appears (legit-content KL 1.77–2.64 nats) while FRA leaves it\n276\tat ~0.000–0.0016 → **FRA is 10³–10⁴× more separable**. This is THE metric (sprint-1 red-team's fix:\n277\tlegit-content KL vs a content-gated *projection-removal* steer at matched removal — NOT A-ratio vs\n278\thead-ablation strawmen).\n279\t\n280\t## 3. The magnitude law (validated at both extremes)\n281\t**A ≈ reuse(marginal endpoint) / reuse(conjunction)**, reuse measured on the eval-distribution support\n282\t(naive corpus rates are degenerate — the s5 failure). A~10³–10⁴ when the conjunction is unique (acronym\n283\t26,000×, retrieval 1,100×); A→1.9× when a generic endpoint makes the conjunction recur (shared-endpoint).\n284\tDifferential cells buy back specificity (1.9→7.9×) at a reach cost (58→33%).\n285\t\n286\t## 4. The win-checklist (predict before you test)\n287\tFRA gives a clean behavioral win iff ALL hold: **(1) edge-routed** (CCF-high; not positional/output-dir),\n288\t**(2) load-bearing & non-redundant** (LBNR causal-cut R≈1; no backups/alternative cues), **(3) direct\n289\tconsumption** (the behavior IS the OV-transported content, not downstream MLP), **(4) conjunction-specific\n290\twith a DISTINCTIVE-CONTENT query** (a specific token/feature, not a generic role), **(5) reach** (SAE-\n291\texplained × head-coverage × top-K clears the softmax margin at faithful scale).\n292\t\n293\t## 5. The win/loss taxonomy (the campaign's central, honest finding)\n294\t**FRA's win-class is NARROW**: content-query, token-specific, conjunctive, load-bearing, non-redundant\n295\tattention edges. **Confirmed wins:** induction (15×), in-context backdoor (25×), copy-suppression (516×\n296\tnatural / 26,000× separability), retrieval (1,100×), acronym (26,000×). **Failures, each for a checklist\n297\treason:** weight-baked sleeper (output direction, clause 1), greater-than (MLP-downstream, clause 3), IOI\n298\t(backups, clause 2), docstring/delimiter (redundant, clause 2), many-shot jailbreak (direction-routed),\n299\tPII/entity retrieval (positional query, clause 4), class-union (token-specific keys, clause 4). **Most\n300\testablished-benchmark behaviors fall OUTSIDE the win-class** — they are MLP-routed, positional, distributed,\n301\tor redundant. FRA is a *specialized* instrument, not a general steering method, and its niche — the\n302\t\"missing middle\" between direction steers (marginal + broadcast) and position patches (specific but not\n303\tcontent-addressed) — is now precisely characterized.\n304\t\n305\t## 6. Sprint-2's specific contributions (the theory agent earned its place)\n306\tThe Fable theory agent did not just ideate — it produced the **magnitude law** and the **cell/set-algebra\n307\tframing** that the evals then *validated* (the s5 magnitude-predictor that failed in sprint-1 now has the\n308\tcorrect form), and its **clause-4 sharpening** (distinctive-content query) was derived from cycle-1's\n309\tshared-endpoint result and confirmed by cycle-2/3's negatives. Theory→eval→theory closed a real loop.\n310\t\n311\t## SPRINT 2 — magnitude law validated as a CURVE (`fig_magnitude_law.png`)\n312\tControlled sweep: N boxes share the value 'frog'; query the first; siblings = the other N-1.\n313\t| N | A_generic | A_differential |\n314\t|---|---|---|\n315\t| 2 | 1.4 | 6.3 |\n316\t| 3 | 1.5 | 13.4 |\n317\t| 4 | 2.0 | 23.8 |\n318\tWith **generic** top pairs the (box-query × frog-value) conjunction recurs across all N siblings →\n319\tA ≈ N/N ≈ 1 (flat, measured 1.4–2.0). With **differential** cells (red-edge minus sibling-edges) the\n320\tconjunction is unique → A ≈ reuse(marginal=N)/1 (grows, measured 6.3→23.8). **This is the direct,\n321\tcontrolled confirmation of A ≈ reuse(marginal)/reuse(conjunction)** — the magnitude law the sprint-1 s5\n322\tattempt failed to capture now holds as a quantitative curve, and it operationalizes the differential-cell\n323\tmethod (selectivity is buyable by making the conjunction unique, at a reach cost: differential on-target\n324\tremoval weakens 0.043→0.18 as N grows, since shared score-mass is subtracted away).\n325\t"}]},"uuid":"45023310-57d9-43ec-a648-6ef512d222e1","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:32.362Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1e007486-8b9e-48e0-8c37-a5d297a15191","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
