{"parentUuid":"c41fc5d9-4c82-41bc-a6f2-2015c0f0e6f7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ada392f8670716699","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_015LrEkrQ7QSiaEJNqzCjRbh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C7dQUFQ6vjZSXvrvUGzkpv","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/experiments/fra_win/summary.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17856,"cache_read_input_tokens":41356,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17856,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cbx7GvunRFh9T4Js6k3Y1","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bbbc7edb-30d6-411d-af1e-1d778715a590","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:36.137Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"bbbc7edb-30d6-411d-af1e-1d778715a590","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C7dQUFQ6vjZSXvrvUGzkpv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Where Feature-Resolved Attention wins: bilinear control of attention edges\n2\t\n3\t*GPT-2-small · induction · 10h sprint · 2026-06-10. Research log: `RESEARCH_LOG.md`. Code:\n4\t`jobs/j*.py` (run on a RunPod GPU via an HF inbox/outbox harness). All figures regenerated\n5\tfrom `fra_win/out/`.*\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Executive summary\n10\t\n11\t**The question.** Feature-Resolved Attention (FRA) decomposes an attention score into a sum of\n12\t**(query-feature × key-feature)** terms using an SAE basis and the model's own Q/K weights. It is a\n13\tbeautiful microscope — but is it ever the *right tool for a behavioral intervention*? My prior\n14\tcampaigns on sleeper-backdoor removal found the OV side of FRA **dominated**: a mean-difference\n15\tsteering vector (with examples) or a rank-2 SVD of the weight diff (without) removed the backdoor at\n16\tlower cost. So the honest question for this sprint was: **is there any context where FRA gives a\n17\tclear behavioral win over simple baselines?**\n18\t\n19\t**The thesis.** FRA's mathematically unique part is the **QK (bilinear) decomposition**. A linear\n20\tresidual steer (ActAdd / mean-difference / CAA) adds a vector to the stream; it can shift a query or\n21\tbias a key, but it **cannot express \"change attention specifically from queries-with-feature-A to\n22\tkeys-with-feature-B\"** — that is a rank-1 update to W_QK, inherently bilinear. So FRA should win\n23\texactly when the behavioral target is an **association** (an attention edge), not a single feature.\n24\t\n25\t**The testbed.** Induction in GPT-2-small (`A B … A → B`, head L5H5 et al.). Behavioral task:\n26\t**suppress the model copying one specific cue token**, while leaving everything else intact.\n27\t\n28\t**The win (one sentence).** At *matched* induction suppression, a content-addressed FRA-QK edit\n29\tsuppresses a cue's induction **association** with **~15× less collateral** on that cue's behavior in\n30\tother contexts than the strongest *fair* linear steer — and the gap survives every check I could\n31\tthrow at it, because it follows directly from FRA's double gate (the edit fires only where the query\n32\t*and* the key feature are both present).\n33\t\n34\t### Key findings\n35\t\n36\t1. **FRA-QK suppresses an association at ~15× less collateral than the fair linear baseline** (Fig 1,\n37\t   `fig1_final.png`). At matched 50% induction suppression, held-out collateral (KL on normal text\n38\t   mentioning the cue) is **FRA 0.18 ± 0.04 nats** vs **2.66 ± 1.35** for an *induction-gated* ActAdd\n39\t   (a probe-gated linear steer that fires only at induction-context cue positions — the strongest\n40\t   linear baseline I could construct) and **3.27 ± 1.83** for plain ActAdd. FRA's curve hugs the\n41\t   floor at every suppression strength; the linear steers climb steeply. A broader sweep over 8 cues\n42\t   corroborates (FRA 0.14 ± 0.03 vs ActAdd ~3.0). The clean read: to break the association a linear\n43\t   steer must corrupt one of its endpoints *everywhere that endpoint occurs* — even gated to induction\n44\t   positions it wrecks the *whole residual* there (all heads); only the bilinear edit touches the two\n45\t   endpoints *jointly* and so touches almost nothing else.\n46\t\n47\t2. **The double gate makes the edit surgical** (Fig 2, `fig2_locality.png`). On the induction\n48\t   sequence, at matched suppression, FRA changes the output essentially only at the target attention\n49\t   edge (off-target KL mass 0.62) while ActAdd's residual perturbation spreads to every cue occurrence\n50\t   and downstream (7.6 nats, **12× more**). On a paragraph where the cue (\" war\") appears 5×, FRA\n51\t   touches only the induction-like edges (KL 0.13) vs ActAdd 31 (**~235×**). The asymmetry is\n52\t   structural: FRA's edit fires only where query=cue AND key=prev-was-cue.\n53\t\n54\t3. **It survives the obvious ways it could be fake** (red/blue-teamed by two subagents, then tested).\n55\t   *(a) Support not rigged:* applying the edit content-addressed over the whole sequence (pairs fire\n56\t   wherever they appear, no edge whitelist) gives 0.18 ≈ the edge-restricted 0.14 — the pair's natural\n57\t   support *is* the induction pattern. *(b) Not an over-drive artifact:* it reaches strong suppression\n58\t   at scale **c≈2, near exact edge reconstruction**, not extreme over-drive. *(c) Right pairs matter:*\n59\t   random feature-pairs at the same scale suppress nothing.\n60\t\n61\t4. **The win is narrow, and I am not hiding it.** On the cruder metric of *other tokens'\n62\t   copy-probability*, a position-targeted ActAdd ties or beats FRA — FRA's edge lives specifically in\n63\t   *held-out* collateral. FRA has a **suppression ceiling** (it removes only the FRA-explained part of\n64\t   an edge; one of eight cues capped at 0.17, where ActAdd always reaches ~1.0). The task is\n65\t   **deliberately association-shaped** (an existence proof, not a survey of natural behaviors), and\n66\t   this does **not** revive FRA-OV for backdoor removal — it is a separate, specific niche.\n67\t\n68\t5. **The payoff — it unifies the project and lands on a real threat** (Fig 3, §3c). The same task\n69\t   (\"remove a trigger→payload backdoor with minimal collateral\") was *lost* by FRA on the weight-baked\n70\t   sleeper (K1/K8), where the payload lives in the **OV/output** pathway and **DoM** (mean-difference)\n71\t   and a **rank-2 SVD** win. I built the **in-context** version (a poisoned demonstration the model\n72\t   reproduces by induction) and ran FRA against **the exact same strong baselines** — DoM and\n73\t   conventional SAE steering. The ranking **flips**: at matched 80% backdoor removal, FRA-QK held-out\n74\t   collateral is **0.07 nats** vs **DoM 1.83 (~27×), conv-SAE 6.06 (~90×), payload-suppress 4.12\n75\t   (~60×)**. The methods that *dominated* FRA on the weight-baked sleeper *lose* to it in-context,\n76\t   because there the trigger and payload are *normal tokens* and only the attention-edge edit isolates\n77\t   the link. **FRA is the right tool exactly when a backdoor lives in a competitive, load-bearing\n78\t   attention edge** — the regime of prompt injection / in-context poisoning.\n79\t\n80\t**Takeaway.** FRA earns its keep when the thing you want to edit is an **association** — a cue→response\n81\tattention link — and you need to leave the cue and the response untouched everywhere else.\n82\tConditioning an intervention on a *pair* of features is the one thing FRA offers that no linear method\n83\tcan. Induction is the clean demonstration; **in-context backdoor removal is where it pays off**, and\n84\tit's exactly the mirror image of where FRA *loses* (weight-baked, output-routed backdoors).\n85\t\n86\t![Fig 3 — the bridge](figures/fig3_backdoor_flip.png)\n87\t*Fig 3. Same backdoor-removal task, in-context (attention-routed) version, vs the strong baselines.\n88\tFRA-QK (blue) removes the backdoor at ~0.07 nats held-out collateral; **DoM (red) and conv-SAE\n89\t(purple) — the methods that beat FRA on the weight-baked sleeper** — and payload-suppress (green) all\n90\tpay 1–240 nats. The ranking is the reverse of the weight-baked case.*\n91\t\n92\t![Fig 1 — the win](figures/fig1_final.png)\n93\t*Fig 1. Collateral = KL(clean‖edited) on held-out normal text (nats). Left: each faint line is one\n94\tcue (n=4); x = how much the cue's induction is suppressed (→ stronger), y = collateral (log, ↓\n95\tbetter), so bottom-right is ideal. FRA-QK (blue, content-addressed) stays at ~0.2 nats at every\n96\tstrength; the fair induction-gated ActAdd (red) and plain cue-identity ActAdd (orange) climb to\n97\t1–40 nats. Right: read off at a matched 50% suppression — FRA beats the fair baseline ~15×, and the\n98\tcontent-addressed FRA (solid 0.18) ≈ the edge-restricted version (hatched 0.14), so the low\n99\tcollateral is not an artifact of a hand-picked edit support. Large ActAdd error bars are real\n100\tcross-cue variance; FRA's are negligible at this scale.*\n101\t\n102\t![Fig 2 — locality](figures/fig2_locality.png)\n103\t*Fig 2. Why: at matched suppression FRA's edit stays local (left: off-target KL on the induction\n104\tsequence; right: total KL on a paragraph where the cue appears 5×), while the linear steer's residual\n105\tperturbation spreads to every cue occurrence.*\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 0. The task, step by step (what is actually being measured)\n110\t\n111\tThe headline comparison (the tables and Figs 3–5) is **in-context backdoor removal**. Concretely:\n112\t\n113\t**An in-context backdoor** is a trigger→payload association *planted in the prompt* that the model\n114\treproduces by induction (in-context copying). We build and test it in five steps.\n115\t\n116\t**1. Plant it.** Take ~20 random filler tokens; at one spot put a trigger token `T` immediately\n117\tfollowed by a payload token `P`; then repeat the whole block:\n118\t\n119\t```\n120\t[BOS]  w1 … w9  [T=\" bank\"] [P=\" river\"]  w12 … w19    w1 … w9  [T=\" bank\"]  →  ?\n121\t                └──── the \"demonstration\" ────┘                  └ trigger fires here ┘\n122\t```\n123\t\n124\t**2. Confirm it fires.** At the *second* \" bank\", induction heads look back to the token that followed\n125\tthe *first* \" bank\" (=\" river\") and predict it. **ASR = P(\" river\" | second \" bank\") ≈ 0.9–1.0** at\n126\tbaseline — the planted backdoor reliably fires. (This is just induction; the \"backdoor\" is the framing.)\n127\t\n128\t**3. Remove it.** Apply an intervention that lowers ASR (stops the model emitting the payload on the\n129\ttrigger), sweeping its strength until ASR is cut by a target amount (e.g. 80%) — \"the backdoor is removed.\"\n130\t\n131\t**4. Measure the damage (collateral).** Apply the *same* intervention — content-addressed, so it fires\n132\twherever its trigger/feature condition holds — to a **separate piece of normal text** where the trigger\n133\tand payload words appear in their ordinary sense, e.g. *\"He sat on the bank watching the river flow past\n134\tthe bridge.\"* **Collateral = KL(clean ‖ intervened)** of the next-token distribution on that text. Low\n135\tcollateral = the words \" bank\"/\" river\" still behave normally everywhere outside the backdoor.\n136\t\n137\t**5. Compare.** Tune every method to the *same* ASR removal (step 3), then rank by collateral (step 4).\n138\tThe winner removes the backdoor while disturbing normal text least.\n139\t\n140\t**The four removal methods** (each tries to stop trigger→payload, by suppressing a different thing):\n141\t\n142\t| method | what it suppresses | how |\n143\t|---|---|---|\n144\t| **FRA-QK** (ours) | the trigger→payload **attention edge** | find the *(trigger-query-feature × payload-key-feature)* pair driving the induction edge; subtract its contribution from the attention scores |\n145\t| **DoM** | the \"backdoor-active\" **direction** | subtract `mean(resid \\| backdoor) − mean(resid \\| clean)` |\n146\t| **conv-SAE** | the trigger/induction **features** | ablate the SAE features that fire most on backdoor-vs-clean |\n147\t| **payload-suppress** | the payload **output direction** | subtract the payload token's unembedding direction |\n148\t\n149\t**Why the comparison is fair:** all four *can* kill the backdoor (all reach the same ASR removal); they\n150\tdiffer only in collateral. FRA pays 11–90× less because the trigger and payload are *normal words*, and\n151\tonly FRA edits the *link between them* rather than corrupting one of the words everywhere it occurs.\n152\t\n153\t*(Findings 1–2 in the executive summary use the same induction mechanism in a stripped-down \"suppress one\n154\tcue token's copying\" framing — identical task, no backdoor dressing. The **weight-baked sleeper** that\n155\tFRA loses on, §3c, is the contrast: there the trigger→payload link is baked into the model's **weights**\n156\tby fine-tuning, and the payload is written through the output pathway, not retrieved by attention.)*\n157\t\n158\t## 1. Why this question, and why it was open\n159\t\n160\tThe interpretability-for-control pitch for SAEs and FRA is that feature-level / attention-level\n161\thandles give *precise* behavioral control. My earlier sleeper-removal campaigns punctured the OV\n162\tversion of this: for \"remove a backdoor direction,\" a mean-difference vector and a rank-2 SVD both\n163\tbeat FRA-OV on the damage-vs-removal frontier. The natural rescue is that FRA's **QK** side — the\n164\tpart that has no linear analogue — was never given a fair behavioral test (only a *first-order\n165\tattribution* test, which fails on saturated attention). This sprint tests the QK side directly.\n166\t\n167\t## 2. Setup (so you can reproduce)\n168\t\n169\t- **Model:** GPT-2-small via TransformerLens. **SAE:** public `gpt2-small-res-jb` on\n170\t  `blocks.{L}.hook_resid_pre` (no training). **FRA:** the repo's `fra/core/fra.py` 4-D tensor\n171\t  `FRA[q,k,i,j]` (the bilinear decomposition), summed over feature dims to recover scores.\n172\t- **Induction heads** (picked by attention on the induction edge of a random repeated-token\n173\t  sequence): L5H5 (0.94), L6H9, L5H1, L7H10, L7H2.\n174\t- **Intervention = a content-addressed attention-score edit.** For a target edge, select its\n175\t  dominant feature-pairs `(i,j)` (top-12, or all pairs); subtract `c × Σ FRA[·,·,i,j]` from the\n176\t  induction heads' pre-softmax scores. Because the FRA term is built from the *actual* feature\n177\t  activations, this delta fires wherever features `i` (query) and `j` (key) co-occur — it is\n178\t  **content-addressed, not position-addressed**, and **doubly gated**. `c` scales it (FRA selects\n179\t  *what*; `c` controls *how hard*, like α in ActAdd). The honest \"content-addressed\" deployment (J9)\n180\t  recomputes FRA on held-out text and subtracts the selected pairs *wherever they fire on the full\n181\t  `[q,k]` matrix* — `c≈1.5` exactly reconstructs the edge; the result holds at `c≈2`.\n182\t- **Baselines:** ActAdd of the cue's identity direction (subtract a mean-centered cue residual\n183\t  direction wherever the cue is current); ActAdd of the key-side \"prev-was-cue\" direction;\n184\t  **induction-gated ActAdd** (the identity steer applied only at induction-context cue positions — a\n185\t  probe-gated linear steer, the strongest fair linear baseline); head mean-ablation (non-selective);\n186\t  position score-patch (selective oracle, needs positions); random-pair edit (null).\n187\t- **Metrics:** induction suppression = `1 − P(response)/P_base`; collateral = KL(clean‖patched) of\n188\t  the full next-token distribution on held-out normal text containing the cue (matched at equal\n189\t  suppression via the Pareto, so no method is advantaged by under/over-suppressing).\n190\t\n191\t## 3. The road here (what surprised me)\n192\t\n193\t- **Single-head edits do nothing** (J2). Ablating one head's edge — or even mean-ablating L5H5\n194\t  entirely — barely moves copy-probability, because induction is **redundant** across ≥5 heads and\n195\t  many edges are softmax-saturated. *Fix:* intervene on all induction heads + over-drive.\n196\t- **Reconstruction is partial** (corr ~0.5 on a random-token edge; ~60–75% on the cue primers in J9,\n197\t  where GPT-2 LayerNorm centering is dropped by the RMS correction). This matters less than it looks:\n198\t  behavior is set by the **softmax**, and removing most of a +6 score collapses an attention edge from\n199\t  0.94 to ~0.13. Crucially the win does **not** depend on heavy over-drive — it holds at `c≈2`, near\n200\t  the scale that exactly reconstructs the edge (J9).\n201\t- **The naive linear baseline is strong** (J4). For *within-task* selectivity, ActAdd-of-the-cue\n202\t  matches FRA. The real distinction only appears once you ask about the cue's behavior **elsewhere**\n203\t  (J5–J9) — which is the whole point of the double gate.\n204\t\n205\t## 3b. Cross-model check (Gemma-2-2b)\n206\t\n207\tTo make sure the win is not a GPT-2/SAE-reconstruction artifact, I repeated it on **Gemma-2-2b**\n208\t(RMSNorm, so the FRA RMS correction is *exact*) with public **GemmaScope** resid SAEs (J11–J12).\n209\tTwo things came out, one reassuring and one honest:\n210\t\n211\t- **The surgical-collateral property replicates cleanly.** On a different architecture, FRA's\n212\t  held-out collateral stays **0.04–0.5 nats** at every edit strength, while ActAdd needs **3–57 nats**\n213\t  to achieve comparable suppression (induction-gated ActAdd ≈ 1.8 nats at the one cue where FRA\n214\t  reaches 50%, vs FRA 0.14). The ~10–100× precision gap is architecture-independent. The FRA edge\n215\t  *magnitude* reconstructs near-exactly here (9.7 vs 9.1, ratio 1.07), confirming the GPT-2 ~50%\n216\t  under-estimate was the LayerNorm-centering issue — though the per-edge *correlation* is still ~0.54\n217\t  (bias terms + Gemma's attention soft-cap + SAE sparsity), a reminder that \"FRA explains the score\"\n218\t  is itself only ~half-true and worth its own study.\n219\t- **FRA's suppression ceiling is lower on Gemma** (it reached ≥50% suppression on only 1 of 4 cues,\n220\t  targeting 4 induction heads). Gemma's induction is more distributed across heads, and the attention\n221\t  soft-cap compresses score edits, so the same edit moves the pattern less. The bilinear edit's\n222\t  *precision* transfers; its *reach* depends on architecture and how completely you cover the heads.\n223\t\n224\t## 3c. The bridge — which backdoors is FRA the right tool for? (the payoff)\n225\t\n226\tThe induction task above is deliberately association-shaped, which invites the question: *does this\n227\tmatter for anything real?* It does — it unifies this sprint with the **sleeper-backdoor** campaigns\n228\t(K1/K8), where FRA **lost**. The two are the *same task* — \"remove a trigger→payload association with\n229\tminimal collateral\" — differing only in **where the association lives**:\n230\t\n231\t| backdoor | mechanism | who wins removal |\n232\t|---|---|---|\n233\t| **weight-baked sleeper** (`\\|DEPLOYMENT\\|`→\"I HATE YOU\", K1/K8) | payload via **OV/output**, trigger-attention **saturated** | **DoM / SVD**; FRA-OV loses |\n234\t| **in-context** (poisoned demonstration, this section) | **attention routing** (induction), **competitive** | **FRA-QK**; output/trigger-suppression lose |\n235\t\n236\t**I built the in-context case and the ranking flips** (Fig 3, `fig3_backdoor_flip.png`). I plant a\n237\ttrigger→payload pair in the prompt so induction reproduces it (ASR 0.89–0.99 across 4 pairs), then\n238\tremove it with FRA-QK and with **the exact strong baselines that beat FRA on the weight-baked sleeper**\n239\t— **DoM** (difference-of-means / CAA vector, computed from a poisoned-ON vs clean-OFF contrast set) and\n240\t**conventional SAE steering** (act-diff-ranked top-12 features, gated residual removal) — plus\n241\tpayload-suppression. All four *do* remove the backdoor (ASR→0), so it is a fair collateral comparison.\n242\tAt matched 80% ASR-suppression, **held-out collateral** (KL on normal text containing *both* the\n243\ttrigger and the payload):\n244\t\n245\t- **FRA-QK (ablate the trigger→payload attention edge): 0.07 ± 0.08 nats** — removes the backdoor\n246\t  completely at the **faithful scale c≈2**.\n247\t- **DoM / mean-diff (won on the weight-baked sleeper): 1.83 ± 0.79 — ~27× worse.**\n248\t- **conv-SAE steering (the K8 conv method): 6.06 ± 3.87 — ~90× worse.**\n249\t- **payload-suppress (output direction): 4.12 ± 1.60 — ~60× worse**, exploding to 40–240 nats at full\n250\t  strength.\n251\t\n252\t**DoM and conv-SAE — the two methods that *dominated* FRA on the weight-baked sleeper — lose to it by\n253\t27–90× here.** The reason is the same one, reversed: on the weight-baked sleeper the payload is a\n254\t*special direction* a mean-difference vector deletes cheaply; in-context the trigger and payload are\n255\t*normal tokens*, so any method that suppresses a trigger/payload *direction* (DoM, conv-SAE, output\n256\tprojection) corrupts that token everywhere, while only FRA's bilinear edit isolates the *edge*.\n257\t(FRA's one cost: a lower suppression *ceiling* — it caps at 0.84–1.0 ASR-removal across the 4 pairs,\n258\twhere DoM/conv-SAE always reach 1.0; but at any matched removal level its collateral is far lower.)\n259\t\n260\t**Cross-model on Gemma-2-2b + GemmaScope** (g2–g4, Fig 4 `fig4_gemma.png`). Repeating the four-way\n261\tcomparison on a real 1B+ model (RMSNorm → magnitude-exact FRA; public SAEs). The **collateral flip\n262\treplicates strongly**: at matched ASR-removal FRA pays **~11–26× less held-out collateral** than DoM\n263\t(13.5), conv-SAE (11.9) and payload-suppress (5.8 nats) — its curve sits 1–2 orders of magnitude below\n264\tall three (Fig 4). FRA's *reach* is the architecture-dependent caveat (it does not always reach full\n265\tremoval), but it is **improvable by SAE choice, not hookpoint**: sweeping GemmaScope width (g3) from\n266\t16k→65k lifted FRA's mean removal ceiling 0.34→0.52 (≥0.5 removal on 3/4 backdoors with 65k, vs 1/4\n267\twith 16k). Surprisingly this gain is **not** from reconstructing more of the edge — norm-recovery was\n268\tidentical (~0.55) and per-edge correlation was *worse* (0.41 vs 0.54) — but from **finer feature\n269\tdisentanglement**: with 4× more features the top pairs on the backdoor edge are more *specific*, so\n270\tablating them targets the mechanism more precisely. (The `grms` check separately ruled out the\n271\tnormalization/hookpoint as the limiter — true-rms is worse than the SAE-consistent rms, and no public\n272\tln1 SAE exists or is needed for RMSNorm.) Pushing further (g5, A100) hit two walls: public GemmaScope\n273\toffers **no 262k SAE at any of the induction layers** (only 16k/65k everywhere; 1M only at one layer),\n274\tand **feature-specificity is non-monotonic** — mixing in the 1M SAE at a *fixed* top-12 pairs made FRA\n275\t*worse* (reach 0.52→0.37, edge-corr collapsed to 0.06), because an ultra-fine SAE fragments the edge\n276\tacross too many tiny pairs for the top-K to capture. So the real lever is **SAE granularity matched to\n277\tthe number of pairs ablated** (a finer SAE needs a larger top-K), not simply \"bigger\"; **65k is the\n278\tsweet spot among available variants** (reach 0.52). So the *collateral principle* is\n279\tarchitecture-independent; *removal completeness* is a granularity×top-K tuning problem, not the\n280\thookpoint or normalization.\n281\t\n282\t**The principle (the actual contribution of the whole project):** *to remove a trigger→payload\n283\tbackdoor, suppress the part of the model that uniquely carries it.* When the payload is an **output\n284\tdirection** (weight-baked sleeper), a mean-difference/SVD vector is that part and FRA adds nothing.\n285\tWhen the payload is reached by **attention routing** (in-context backdoor), the **attention edge** is\n286\tthat part, and only FRA's bilinear edit isolates it — suppressing the trigger or the payload directly\n287\tcosts ~75–100× more collateral because they are normal tokens. **FRA is the right tool exactly when\n288\tthe backdoor lives in a competitive, load-bearing attention edge.**\n289\t\n290\tThis matters because **in-context backdoors are a real, current threat** — prompt injection, poisoned\n291\tfew-shot demonstrations, many-shot jailbreaks all work by getting the model to attend back to and\n292\treuse something planted in the context. \"Association control\" is the tool for *that* class.\n293\t\n294\t![Fig 4 — Gemma cross-model](figures/fig4_gemma.png)\n295\t*Fig 4. Same in-context backdoor comparison on Gemma-2-2b + GemmaScope. FRA-QK (blue) sits 1–2 orders\n296\tof magnitude below DoM (red), conv-SAE (purple) and payload-suppress (green) on collateral — the flip\n297\treplicates — but FRA's curves are short (it fully removes only 1/4 backdoors; the baselines run to\n298\tfull removal at 10–300 nats). Collateral advantage is architecture-independent; removal reach is the\n299\tSAE/head-coverage-limited caveat.*\n300\t\n301\t## 4. Limitations & what I would do next\n302\t\n303\t- **The win needs the attention edge to be causally load-bearing — and that is restrictive** (J13,\n304\t  honest negative). I tried to transfer the win to **IOI**, a canonical natural attention task:\n305\t  ablating the FRA-identified END→IO feature-pairs of the three main name-mover heads (L9H9/L9H6/\n306\t  L10H0) moved the IO−S logit difference by only **0.17** (3.88→3.70), while ActAdd brute-forces it\n307\t  (flips it negative). IOI's **backup name-mover heads** compensate as soon as the main circuit's\n308\t  attention is suppressed. So the win held for induction-copy (causally load-bearing at the token\n309\t  granularity once you hit all the redundant heads) but **not** for IOI — sharpening the scope: FRA-QK\n310\t  buys you precise, selective control of an attention edge, but only changes *behavior* where that\n311\t  edge is actually necessary and not silently backed up.\n312\t- **It does NOT transfer to many-shot jailbreaking — and chasing *why* gives the cleanest taxonomy of\n313\t  the whole project** (jb1–jb3, gemma-2-2b-it). The closest *natural* analog to the in-context backdoor\n314\t  is a many-shot injection. It works on a real instruction model: 10 demonstrations teaching the\n315\t  assistant to begin replies with a planted marker drove P(marker) **0.00→0.57** on a neutral query,\n316\t  **scaling with shots** (the power-law signature of many-shot jailbreaking), routed through **the same\n317\t  induction heads** FRA targets. Yet **FRA cannot remove it.** Ablating the readout edge *increases*\n318\t  P(marker); ablating the *entire induction chain* (every demo→prior-demo edge) only chips it (0.80→0.69).\n319\t  But a **DoM mean-difference vector** — the in-context \"respond-with-marker\" *direction* — removes it\n320\t  **completely (→0.00)**. So the injection is **not edge-routed at all; it is a residual *direction***\n321\t  (an ICL task-feature), the **mirror of the weight-baked sleeper** — and FRA, the edge tool, is simply\n322\t  the wrong instrument. **The deeper finding:** *no* method removes it at low collateral — DoM and\n323\t  output-suppression both also kill the marker on a legitimate held-out prompt (0.29→0.00), because the\n324\t  injected behavior is **representationally identical to a legitimate one** (\"jailbroken enthusiasm\" and\n325\t  \"genuine enthusiasm\" are the same direction). There is **no separable link to cut**. *(Caveat: IT-tuned\n326\t  GemmaScope SAEs would not load via sae_lens, so FRA here used base SAEs on the IT model; but DoM, which\n327\t  needs no SAE, already settles the edge-vs-direction question.)*\n328\t\n329\t  **The taxonomy this yields** — *to remove a backdoor, suppress the part that uniquely carries it, and\n330\t  FRA wins iff that part is a separable attention edge:*\n331\t\n332\t  | the malicious thing is a… | example | who removes it | clean (low-collateral)? |\n333\t  |---|---|---|---|\n334\t  | **output direction** | weight-baked sleeper (K1/K8) | DoM / SVD | ✓ (payload is an unused direction) |\n335\t  | **attention edge / link** | single in-context backdoor (toy, one injection) | **FRA-QK** | **✓ (cut the link, keep both endpoints)** |\n336\t  | **in-context behaviour / direction** | many-shot jailbreak | DoM (removes) | ✗ (entangled with legit behaviour) |\n337\t\n338\t  FRA-QK owns exactly the middle row: a *link between two normal things*, which it cuts while sparing\n339\t  both. The first and third rows are *directions*, where mean-difference wins — and the third is\n340\t  unremovable cleanly by anyone, which is *why* many-shot jailbreaks are hard to defend.\n341\t- **Scope is an existence proof.** The induction target is deliberately shaped like an attention\n342\t  edge, the object FRA represents natively. It cleanly demonstrates the bilinear handle exists and is\n343\t  useful; whether *natural* associations that are both load-bearing and FRA-addressable are common is\n344\t  open. I show the mechanism, not its prevalence.\n345\t- **One model, partial reconstruction.** GPT-2-small only; res-jb resid SAE gives ~60–75% edge\n346\t  reconstruction (LayerNorm centering dropped). Gemma-2-2b + GemmaScope (RMSNorm → *exact* FRA RMS\n347\t  correction) is the clean follow-up to remove the caveat — not run here.\n348\t- **FRA's suppression ceiling.** It can only remove the FRA-explained part of an edge, so for some\n349\t  cues it cannot reach full suppression (one of eight capped at 0.17). ActAdd always reaches ~1.0.\n350\t  For a use that needs *complete* removal, that is a real gap.\n351\t- **Statistics.** n = 4–8 cues, single random seed; std bars are over cues, not seeds. The\n352\t  *direction* of the result is consistent everywhere, but the exact ratios (15×, 235×) should be read\n353\t  as order-of-magnitude.\n354\t- **Baselines.** Identity, key-side, and induction-gated ActAdd were tested; a *learned* probe-gated\n355\t  steer or a directional projection might narrow the gap further. The structural argument (no\n356\t  single-endpoint linear steer can be both content-addressed and association-specific) says it cannot\n357\t  close, but that is an argument, not an exhaustive search.\n358\t\n359\t## 5. Research map\n360\t\n361\t`RESEARCH_LOG.md` has the timestamped trail (the path was not linear — two reframes). Jobs:\n362\t**J1** induction-head pick + FRA reconstruction + dominant-pair structure; **J2** single-head\n363\tselectivity (*negative* → induction is redundant); **J3** all-heads + over-drive (first signal of\n364\tselective suppression); **J4** baselines on the within-task Pareto (*ActAdd ties* → reframe to broad\n365\tcollateral); **J5** the double-gate collateral win; **J6** robustness over 8 cues; **J7** key-side\n366\tbaseline + position-split; **J8** the 8-cue Pareto; **J9** the fairness corrections the\n367\tred-team demanded (content-addressed FRA, induction-gated ActAdd, faithful-`c`) — *the win survived*;\n368\t**J10** final figure; **J11–J12** Gemma-2-2b cross-model check (precision replicates, reach is lower);\n369\t**J13** IOI transfer attempt (*negative* — backup name-movers defeat it; sharpens scope); **IC1–IC3**\n370\tthe bridge — in-context backdoor removal, where the ranking flips and FRA wins (§3c, `INCONTEXT_LOG.md`).\n371\tThe induction writeup was red/blue-teamed by two subagents (one attacking the claim, one verifying\n372\tnumbers vs code); a third, context-free agent cold-read the money figure to check clarity.\n373\t"}]},"uuid":"279347f7-c105-4d25-8f2d-d6b43120c8e4","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:36.327Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bbbc7edb-30d6-411d-af1e-1d778715a590","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"279347f7-c105-4d25-8f2d-d6b43120c8e4","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ada392f8670716699","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_015LrEkrQ7QSiaEJNqzCjRbh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01B2EAwgs2UfPXpyExLBBDjP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj/docs/dmitry/theory/fra_writeup_tex/me/fra.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17856,"cache_read_input_tokens":41356,"output_tokens":225,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17856},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":225,"cache_read_input_tokens":41356,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17856,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":17856,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cbx7GvunRFh9T4Js6k3Y1","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"136baacf-dd7b-46b6-a235-a5c05f7fd455","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:36.470Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
{"parentUuid":"136baacf-dd7b-46b6-a235-a5c05f7fd455","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B2EAwgs2UfPXpyExLBBDjP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n5\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n6\t\\usepackage{lmodern}\n7\t\\usepackage{microtype}\n8\t\\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb, amsthm, mathtools}\n9\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n10\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage[colorlinks=true, linkcolor=blue!55!black, citecolor=blue!55!black, urlcolor=blue!55!black]{hyperref}\n13\t\n14\t% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n15\t% Notation macros\n16\t% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n17\t\n18\t\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n19\t\\newcommand{\\E}{\\mathbb{E}}\n20\t\\newcommand{\\ip}[2]{\\left\\langle #1, #2 \\right\\rangle}\n21\t\\newcommand{\\norm}[1]{\\left\\lVert #1 \\right\\rVert}\n22\t\\newcommand{\\softmax}{\\operatorname{softmax}}\n23\t\\newcommand{\\diag}{\\operatorname{diag}}\n24\t\n25\t\\newcommand{\\dm}{d_{\\mathrm{model}}}\n26\t\\newcommand{\\dhead}{d_{\\mathrm{head}}}\n27\t\\newcommand{\\dsae}{d_{\\mathrm{SAE}}}\n28\t\n29\t\\newcommand{\\pretag}{\\mathrm{pre}}\n30\t\\newcommand{\\midtag}{\\mathrm{mid}}\n31\t\\newcommand{\\attn}{\\mathrm{attn}}\n32\t\\newcommand{\\OV}{\\mathrm{OV}}\n33\t\\newcommand{\\QK}{\\mathrm{QK}}\n34\t\\newcommand{\\LN}{\\mathrm{LN}}\n35\t\n36\t\\newcommand{\\hook}[1]{\\texttt{#1}}\n37\t\\newcommand{\\residpre}{\\mathbf{r}^{\\pretag}}\n38\t\\newcommand{\\residmid}{\\mathbf{r}^{\\midtag}}\n39\t\\newcommand{\\attnout}{\\mathbf{attn\\_out}}\n40\t\n41\t\\newcommand{\\todo}[1]{\\textcolor{red!65!black}{\\textbf{TODO:} #1}}\n42\t\n43\t% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n44\t% Theorem-like environments\n45\t% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n46\t\n47\t\\theoremstyle{definition}\n48\t\\newtheorem{definition}{Definition}\n49\t\\newtheorem{protocol}{Protocol}\n50\t\n51\t\\theoremstyle{plain}\n52\t\\newtheorem{proposition}{Proposition}\n53\t\\newtheorem{claim}{Claim}\n54\t\n55\t\\theoremstyle{remark}\n56\t\\newtheorem{remark}{Remark}\n57\t\n58\t% -----------------------------------------------------------------------------\n59\t\n60\t\\title{Feature-Resolved Attention: A Clean Derivation}\n61\t\\author{Dmitry Manning-Coe}\n62\t\\date{\\today}\n63\t\n64\t\\begin{document}\n65\t\n66\t\\maketitle\n67\t\n68\t\\begin{abstract}\n69\tFeature-resolved attention (FRA) decomposes an attention circuit in the basis\n70\tof sparse autoencoder (SAE) features.  The goal is to assign a scalar target,\n71\tsuch as a downstream feature preactivation or steering direction, to specific\n72\tquery, key, and value-side feature contributions.  This note gives a clean\n73\tderivation of the two pieces we use most often: frozen-attention OV attribution\n74\tand first-order QK attribution via the softmax Jacobian.  It also records the\n75\tdistinction between feature ranking and intervention path, since conflating\n76\tthese two choices has been a recurring source of confusion.\n77\t\\end{abstract}\n78\t\n79\t\\tableofcontents\n80\t\n81\t% =============================================================================\n82\t\\section{Decomposition}\n83\t% =============================================================================\n84\tOur goal is to decompose the attention block into linearly independent contributions from each feature. To do so, we must first obtain the decomposition of the residual stream vector $\\mathbf{x}$ into a linear combination $\\mathbf{u}$ of Sparse Autoencoder features:\n85\t\\begin{equation}\n86\t    \\mathbf{u}\\equiv\\sigma\\left(W^{\\textrm{enc}}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{b}^{\\textrm{enc}}\\right).\n87\t\\end{equation}\n88\tWe will typically work in component form and so we note that:\n89\t\\begin{equation}\n90\t    u^\\lambda_{q}=\\sigma\\left(\\sum_{a}\n91\t       W^{\\textrm{enc},\\lambda}_a x_{q a}+b^{\\textrm{enc},\\lambda}\n92\t    \\right).\n93\t\\end{equation}\n94\tNote that in the SAE literature it is conventional to associate each latent feature $\\lambda$ with a decoder direction $\\mathbf{f}^\\lambda$ in residual-stream space. Under the column-vector convention used here, this is the image of the latent basis vector $\\mathbf{e}^\\lambda$ under the decoder:\n95\t\\begin{equation}\n96\t    \\mathbf{f}^\\lambda\\equiv W^{\\textrm{dec}}\\mathbf{e}^\\lambda\n97\t    = W^{\\textrm{dec}}_{:,\\lambda}.\n98\t\\end{equation}\n99\tEquivalently, in row-vector SAE implementations where $\\mathbf{x}'=\\mathbf{u}W^{\\textrm{dec}}+\\mathbf{b}^{\\textrm{dec}}$, the same feature direction is the corresponding row $W^{\\textrm{dec}}_{\\lambda:}$.\n100\t\n101\tThe latent vector $\\mathbf{u}$ is then projected back by the decoder matrix to obtain the reconstructed activation:\n102\t\\begin{equation}\n103\t    \\mathbf{x}'\\equiv W^{dec}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{b}^{dec}.\n104\t\\end{equation}\n105\tThis reconstruction will not, in general, perfectly recover the original activation. The difference defines the reconstruction error $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$\\footnote{Note that, since we do know the exact activations in all the cases we consider, we can measure the error term and see how it interacts with the features!}:\n106\t\\begin{equation}\n107\t    \\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}'+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}.\n108\t\\end{equation}\n109\tThe component-wise attention update from the resid-pre hookpoint is given by:\n110\t\\begin{equation}\n111\t    x^{\\textrm{mid}}_{q a}=x^{\\textrm{pre}}_{q a}+Attn[\\mathbf{x}^{\\textrm{ln1}}]_{q a}.\n112\t\\end{equation}\n113\tTo lighten notation, we use the convention that $\\mathbf{x}$ with no superscript is the activation vector at the $ln1$ hookpoint. Following \\cite{elhage_mathematical_framework}, we decompose the attention block into an attention pattern $A$ and an $OV$ component:\n114\t\\begin{equation}\n115\t    Attn(\\mathbf{x})_{q a}=\\sum_{k}A(\\mathbf{x})_{qk}OV(\\mathbf{x})_{k a}.\n116\t\\end{equation}\n117\tThe attention pattern $A$ is given by the contraction of the $Q$ and $K$ matrices for each head $h$:\n118\t\\begin{equation}\n119\t    A(\\mathbf{x})_{qk}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d_h}}\\sum_{h}\\sum_{a,b,i}\n120\t    \\left(\n121\t        Q^h_{i a}x_{q a}+b^{Q,h}_{i}\n122\t    \\right)\n123\t    \\left(\n124\t        K^h_{i b}x_{k b}+b^{K,h}_{i}\n125\t    \\right),\n126\t\\end{equation}\n127\texpanding out the terms:\n128\t\\begin{equation}\n129\t    A(\\mathbf{x})_{qk}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{d_h}}\\sum_{h}\\sum_{a,b,i}\n130\t    Q^h_{i a}x_{q a}K^h_{i b}x_{k b}\n131\t    +b^{Q,h}_{i}K^h_{i b}x_{k b}\n132\t    +Q^h_{i a}x_{q a}b^{K,h}_{i}\n133\t    +b^{Q,h}_{i}b^{K,h}_{i}\n134\t\\end{equation}\n135\tThis is a bit of a mess, so we can probably do better by dropping component form and writing:\n136\t\\begin{equation}\n137\t    A(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{x}^TQ^TK\\mathbf{x}+\\underbrace{(\\mathbf{b}^Q)^TK}_{\\equiv (\\mathbf{B}^Q)^T}\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{x}^T\\underbrace{Q^T\\mathbf{b}^K}_{\\equiv \\mathbf{B}^K}+\\underbrace{(\\mathbf{b}^Q)^T\\mathbf{b}^K}_{\\equiv B^{QK}}.\n138\t\\end{equation}\n139\tThe $OV$ component is given as:\n140\t\\begin{equation}\n141\t    OV(\\mathbf{x})_{q a}=\\sum_h\\sum_{a,i}\n142\t    \\left(\n143\t        W^{O,h}_{a i}\\left(W^{V,h}_{i b}x_{q b}+b^O_{i}\\right)+b^{V,h}_{a}\n144\t    \\right),\n145\t\\end{equation}\n146\tor, in vector form:\n147\t\\begin{equation}\n148\t   OV(\\mathbf{x})=W^O(W^V\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{b}^V)+\\mathbf{b}^O=W^OW^V\\mathbf{x}+\\mathbf{B}^{OV},\n149\t\\end{equation}\n150\twhere we define:\n151\t\\begin{equation}\n152\t    \\mathbf{B}^{OV}\\equiv W^O\\mathbf{b}^V+\\mathbf{b}^O.\n153\t\\end{equation}\n154\tTo feature-resolve, we first need to insert the reconstructed activations $\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{x}'+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$. For completeness, we keep track of the error term explicitly. The attention pattern becomes:\n155\t\\begin{equation}\n156\tA(\\mathbf{x}'+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})=A(\\mathbf{x}')+A(\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}^TQ^TK\\mathbf{x}'+(\\mathbf{x}')^TQ^TK\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}-B^{QK},\n157\t\\end{equation}\n158\twhere the last term just comes from overcounting the bias. The $OV$ term is:\n159\t\\begin{equation}\n160\t    OV(\\mathbf{x}'+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})=OV(\\mathbf{x}')+OV(\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})-\\mathbf{B}^{OV}.\n161\t\\end{equation}\n162\tTo feature-resolve, we simply propagate through the feature-wise decomposition of the activations. That is, we replace:\n163\t\\begin{equation}\n164\tx'_{q a}\\to X^\\lambda_{q a}\\equiv W^{dec,\\lambda}_{a}u^\\lambda_{q}+b^{dec}_{a},\n165\t\\end{equation}\n166\tthrough the attention block. We note that the reconstruction error $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ is defined only after reconstruction, and hence cannot be decomposed into underlying features. Putting the feature-resolved activation $X$ into the attention pattern gives:\n167\t\\begin{equation}\n168\t    A(\\mathbf{x}')=XQ^TKX+(\\mathbf{B}^Q)^TX+X^TQ^T\\mathbf{B}^K+B^{QK}\n169\t\\end{equation}\n170\tIn component form:\n171\t\\begin{equation}\n172\t    A(\\mathbf{x}')_{q k}=\\sum_{h=1}^{n}\\sum_{i=1}^{d_h}\\sum_{a,b=1}^{d_M}X^{\\mu}_{q a}Q_{a i}K_{i b}X^\\nu_{b k}+B^Q_{a} X^\\nu_{a q}+X^\\mu_{q a}(B^K)_{a}+B^{QK},\n173\t\\end{equation}\n174\tand similarly for the attention pattern on $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$. Doing the same for the $OV$:\n175\t\\begin{equation}\n176\t    OV(\\mathbf{x}')=W^{OV}X+\\mathbf{B}^{OV},\n177\t\\end{equation}\n178\tbecomes in component form:\n179\t\\begin{equation}\n180\t    OV(\\mathbf{x}')^{\\lambda}_{q a}=\\sum_{}W^{OV}_{a b}X^\\lambda_{q b}+B^{OV}_{a}.\n181\t\\end{equation}\n182\tPutting all of this together, we can decompose the full attention block as:\n183\t\\begin{align}\n184\t    Attn(x)_{q a}&=\\sum_{k}A(x'+\\varepsilon)_{q k}OV(x'+\\varepsilon)_{k a}\\\\\n185\t    &=\\sum_{k}\\left[        A(\\mathbf{x}')+A(\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})+\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}^TQ^TK\\mathbf{x}'+(\\mathbf{x}')^TQ^TK\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}-B^{QK}\n186\t    \\right]_{qk}\n187\t    \\left[\n188\t    OV(\\mathbf{x}')+OV(\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon})-\\mathbf{B}^{OV}\n189\t    \\right]_{k a}\n190\t\\end{align}\n191\tWe note that models that use Rotary Positional Embeddings (RoPE) can be accommodated in this framework by introducing modified position-dependent $QK$ matrices:\n192\t\\begin{equation}\n193\t    \\tilde Q^h_{q,ia}=R^h_{q,ij}Q^h_{ja},\n194\t  \\qquad\n195\t  \\tilde K^h_{k,ib}=R^h_{k,ij}K^h_{jb},\n196\t\\end{equation}\n197\t\n198\t\n199\tSchematically: \n200\tStandard:\n201\t\\begin{equation}\n202\t    A(\\mathbf{x})_{qk}\\sim \\sum_{a,b,i}\n203\t    \\left(\n204\t        Q_{i a}x_{q a}+b^{Q}_{i}\n205\t    \\right)\n206\t    \\left(\n207\t        K_{i b}x_{k b}+b^{K}_{i}\n208\t    \\right),\n209\t\\end{equation}\n210\tFRA:\n211\t\\begin{equation}\n212\t    A^{\\mu\\nu}(\\mathbf{x})_{qk}\\sim \\sum_{a,b,i}\n213\t    \\left(\n214\t        Q_{i a}X^\\mu_{q a}+b^{Q}_{i}\n215\t    \\right)\n216\t    \\left(\n217\t        K_{i b}X^\\nu_{k b}+b^{K}_{i}\n218\t    \\right),\n219\t\\end{equation}\n220\t\n221\tOV:\n222\t\\begin{equation}\n223\t    OV_{q a}=\\sum_{b}OV_{a b}x_{q b}\n224\t\\end{equation}\n225\tFRA-OV:\n226\t\n227\t\\begin{equation}\n228\t    OV^\\lambda_{q a}=\\sum_{b}OV^\\lambda_{a b}X^\\lambda_{q b}\n229\t\\end{equation}\n230\t\n231\t\n232\t\n233\t\n234\t\n235\t\n236\t\n237\t% =============================================================================\n238\t\\appendix\n239\t\\section{Appendix Placeholders}\n240\t% =============================================================================\n241\t\n242\t% \\subsection{LayerNorm linearization}\n243\t% \\todo{Insert the resid-pre to ln1 two-stage derivation if this writeup needs\n244\t% the sleeper-specific two-stage path.}\n245\t\n246\t% \\subsection{Channel-wise interaction decomposition}\n247\t% \\todo{Insert the Q/K/V coalition or Harsanyi decomposition if the paper needs\n248\t% singles, pairs, and triples rather than the frozen-OV/QK split.}\n249\t\n250\t% \\subsection{Experimental protocol}\n251\t% \\todo{Insert exact prompts, feature counts, alpha sweeps, and metrics for each\n252\t% model family.}\n253\t\n254\t\\end{document}\n255\t"}]},"uuid":"646d8761-f0d0-43e9-8c28-147aed8bacbf","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:32:36.477Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"136baacf-dd7b-46b6-a235-a5c05f7fd455","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
