{"parentUuid":"ae1835bc-37c9-4757-9d82-4758fdb08f17","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"2c8a0751-1493-4563-8cf9-c724afe36b6d","agentId":"ada392f8670716699","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6qU724xRb5yWtuavERjxB","type":"tool_result","content":"82\t\\begin{description}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=2pt]\n83\t  \\item[Claim 1 (the win).] For a behavior carried by a content-query~$\\times$~content-key attention edge, an FRA cell-edit suppresses the behavior at $10^3$--$10^4\\times$ lower collateral than the strongest \\emph{fair} linear baseline---a content-gated projection-removal steer---measured as legitimate-content KL at matched on-target removal (Sec.~\\ref{sec:win}, Fig.~\\ref{fig:sprint2}). The previously-reported $A$-ratios against head-ablation were measured against strawmen; the decisive fair comparison is the linear steer, and FRA wins it by orders of magnitude.\n84\t  \\item[Claim 2 (the magnitude law).] The advantage obeys $A \\approx \\reuse(\\text{marginal endpoint})/\\reuse(\\text{conjunction})$, reuse measured on the evaluation distribution. We confirm it as a controlled curve (Sec.~\\ref{sec:magnitude}, Fig.~\\ref{fig:magnitude}).\n85\t  \\item[Claim 3 (the boundary).] A five-clause checklist predicts the clean win, and \\emph{the win-class is narrow}. Most established-benchmark behaviors fall outside it, each isolating one clause (Sec.~\\ref{sec:boundary}, Fig.~\\ref{fig:sprint2}).\n86\t\\end{description}\n87\t"}]},"uuid":"d8f266ed-e648-4556-85e4-a155b8501bd4","timestamp":"2026-06-11T20:38:14.242Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ae1835bc-37c9-4757-9d82-4758fdb08f17","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/dmitrymanning-coe/Documents/Research/FRA/fra_proj","sessionId":"8a660228-bde0-4e60-bfe2-aafb33fe0b4d","version":"2.1.170","gitBranch":"autoresearch/fra-behavioral-win","slug":"moonlit-churning-gem"}
